OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026", "title": "Critical honeycomb chords with prescribed boundary endpoints", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026/main.pdf", "family": "237", "family_title": "The three-quarter exponent for honeycomb self-avoiding walk", "abstract_html": "Critical self-avoiding walks between prescribed, macroscopically separated boundary ports of a regular honeycomb hexagon have length \\(R^{4/3+o(1)}\\) in probability, where <i>R</i> is the scale of the hexagon. We prove the corresponding statements for half-plane arches, parallel cuts and nonparallel pure cuts. The half-plane law also has mean length \\(R^{4/3+o(1)}\\). A separate strip argument gives endpoint mean laws on one density-one set of heights and in an aligned, critically weighted mixture of all even heights in a macroscopic interval.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1 (Prescribed boundary endpoints)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), \\( \\mathbb P_{D_R;a_R,b_R} \\{R^{4/3-\\varepsilon}\\le L(\\gamma)\\le R^{4/3+\\varepsilon}\\} \\longrightarrow1. \\) The same conclusion holds:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the regular honeycomb lattice embedded as the centers of the triangles of an equilateral triangular tiling of side one. Neighboring centers are joined across a common triangle edge.</p><p class=\"li\">A port is the midpoint of a boundary edge of a domain made of whole triangles. A path enters and leaves normally at its ports, visits only interior centers, and visits no center twice.</p><p class=\"li\">The length \\(L(\\gamma)\\) is the number of visited centers. Ports carry no weight, and\n  \\[\n  w(\\gamma)=\\kappa^{L(\\gamma)},\\qquad\n  \\kappa=(2\\cos(\\pi/8))^{-1}=(2+\\sqrt2)^{-1/2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For boundary ports \\(a,b\\) of a domain \\(D\\),\n  \\[\n  K_D(a,b)=\\sum_{\\gamma:a\\to b\\text{ in }D}w(\\gamma).\n  \\]\n  When this mass is positive and finite, normalization defines the critical chord law \\(\\mathbb P_{D;a,b}\\). A chord has no other boundary exit.</p><p class=\"li\">A pure cut follows one of the three tiling-edge directions; its admissible translates bound whole triangular rows. Distances refer to the fixed embedding.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D_R\\) be convex pure lattice hexagons which, after scaling by \\(R\\), converge to a regular hexagon. Let \\(a_R,b_R\\) be boundary ports whose scaled limits are distinct points in relative interiors of sides.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_{D_R;a_R,b_R}\n \\{R^{4/3-\\varepsilon}\\le L(\\gamma)\\le R^{4/3+\\varepsilon}\\}\n \\longrightarrow1.\n\\]</p><p>The same conclusion holds:</p><p class=\"li\">in a full half-plane, for prescribed boundary ports at distance \\(R\\);</p><p class=\"li\">in a strip of height comparable to \\(R\\), with one prescribed port on each opposite boundary cut and mutual distance \\(O(R)\\);</p><p class=\"li\">in a wedge of angle \\(\\pi/3\\) or \\(2\\pi/3\\) formed by two nonparallel pure cuts, with one prescribed port on each ray, each endpoint having clearance comparable to \\(R\\) from the other cut and distance \\(O(R)\\) from the apex.</p><p>Constants are uniform on fixed compact sets of these nondegenerate scaled geometries. In each case the normalizing mass is at least \\(cR^{-5/4}\\).</p><p>Throughout the outline, \\(f(R)=R^{a+o(1)}\\) means that, for every fixed \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nR^{a-\\epsilon}\\le f(R)\\le R^{a+\\epsilon}\n\\]\nat all sufficiently large scales, with fixed geometric parameters chosen first. A one-sided use retains only the corresponding inequality. Suprema of atoms are taken on their actual support lattices; off-lattice atoms are zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>Propositions 7, 11, and 12 cover, respectively, identical support lines, opposite parallel support lines, and distinct nonparallel support lines. These are the three possibilities for the endpoint sides of the hexagons.</p><p>In each finite hexagon, Proposition 3 gives normalizing mass at least \\(cR^{-5/4}\\). Each corresponding full half-plane, strip, or wedge argument bounds both absolute length exceptions by \\(o(R^{-5/4})\\). Restricting to the hexagon only decreases those exceptions, so division by its connector mass proves the result.</p><p>The same propositions prove the stated full half-plane, strip, and wedge conclusions. Compactness in their geometric constructions makes constants uniform over the specified nondegenerate scaled families.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Theorem 3.2 (A source and a macroscopic terminal interval)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( L(\\gamma)=R^{4/3+o(1)} \\quad\\text{in probability}. \\) The same conclusion holds when the source is summed over a separated macroscopic side-interior interval.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the lattice, port, length, and critical-weight conventions of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">In the hexagons \\(D_R\\) of Theorem 1, fix a source port tending after scaling to a side-interior point.</p><p class=\"li\">Let the terminal ports form a macroscopic interval converging to a compact side-interior interval at positive distance from the source limit.</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize critical weights over all chords from the fixed source to that terminal interval.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nL(\\gamma)=R^{4/3+o(1)}\n\\quad\\text{in probability}.\n\\]\nThe same conclusion holds when the source is summed over a separated macroscopic side-interior interval. For each fixed source, the normalizer is comparable to \\(R^{-1/4}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The diameter bound of Proposition 1 gives the normalizing upper bound \\(CR^{-1/4}\\). Sum Proposition 3 over order-\\(R\\) terminal ports for the lower bound \\(cR^{-1/4}\\).</p><p>It suffices to treat \\(0&lt;\\epsilon&lt;1\\). For opposite inward normals, these chords are a restriction of height-\\(h\\) bridges, \\(h\\asymp R\\), retaining a fixed fraction of \\(B_h\\). Proposition 4 gives concentration.</p><p>Otherwise apply Proposition 6. For an input event of mass \\(E=p_0R^{-1/4}\\), its fixed-source outputs satisfy\n\\[\n\\mu_a(N_s&gt;0)\n\\ge\\frac{(\\int N_s\\,d\\mu_a)^2}{\\int N_s^2\\,d\\mu_a}\n\\ge cp_0^2R^{-1/4}.\n\\]\nAll outputs have endpoint separation order \\(R\\), so their total mass is at most \\(CR^{-1/4}\\).</p><p>For input lengths below \\(R^{4/3-\\epsilon}\\), choose any fixed number \\(M\\) of distinct exponents\n\\[\n\\gamma_j\\in(1-\\epsilon/3,1),\\qquad s_j=R^{\\gamma_j}.\n\\]\nChoose fixed \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) small enough that the \\(M\\) output length windows are disjoint and attached lengths dominate the short input. Proposition 6's lower constant is independent of this fixed \\(\\delta\\). Thus\n\\[\nMcp_0^2\\le C+o(1).\n\\]\nSend \\(R\\to\\infty\\), then \\(M\\to\\infty\\), to get \\(p_0\\to0\\).</p><p>For input lengths above \\(R^{4/3+\\epsilon}\\), use both free landings at scale \\(cR\\). Proposition 6 gives output mass at least\n\\[\nc\\frac{R^3E^2}{R^{7/4}}\n=cp_0^2R^{3/4}.\n\\]\nAll outputs remain long and lie in one size-\\(O(R)\\) convex pure domain. Proposition 1(4) bounds their mass by\n\\[\nR^{-4/3-\\epsilon}R^{25/12+o(1)}\n=o(R^{3/4}).\n\\]\nHence \\(p_0\\to0\\) also for the upper tail.</p><p>The bounds are uniform over a permitted source interval. Sum exceptional masses and normalizers over that interval for the source-summed assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Theorem 9.1 (Every-gap half-plane mean length)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "As each integer \\(g\\to\\infty\\), \\( L(\\gamma)=g^{4/3+o(1)} \\quad\\text{in probability},\\qquad \\mathbb E L(\\gamma)=g^{4/3+o(1)}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the critical honeycomb port convention of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K_g\\) be the critical mass of half-plane arches between two prescribed boundary ports separated by the integer gap \\(g\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Give an arch \\(\\gamma\\) probability \\(\\kappa^{L(\\gamma)}/K_g\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>As each integer \\(g\\to\\infty\\),\n\\[\nL(\\gamma)=g^{4/3+o(1)}\n\\quad\\text{in probability},\\qquad\n\\mathbb E L(\\gamma)=g^{4/3+o(1)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 16 gives\n\\[\nK_g\\mathbb EL\\le g^{1/12+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThe calibrated lower bound \\(K_g\\ge cg^{-5/4}\\) from Proposition 13 yields\n\\[\n\\mathbb EL\\le g^{4/3+o(1)}.\n\\]\nMarkov's inequality gives the upper length-probability tail.</p><p>For the lower tail, every arch has diameter at least a fixed multiple of \\(g\\). Sum Proposition 13(iii) over dyadic diameter bins starting there, with a slightly smaller exponent slack to absorb unit and endpoint conventions. Its short-length mass is superpolynomially small compared with \\(K_g\\). Therefore the short-length probability tends to zero and\n\\[\n\\mathbb EL\\ge g^{4/3-o(1)}.\n\\]\nThis proves both assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "Corollary 9.5 (Confined macroscopic-gap arches)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every integer \\(h\\to\\infty\\), \\( L(\\gamma)=h^{4/3+o(1)} \\quad\\text{in probability},\\qquad \\mathbb E L(\\gamma)=h^{4/3+o(1)}. \\) This law sums over terminal ports before normalization and is distinct from the …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a half-plane boundary source in the critical honeycomb port convention.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a sufficiently large fixed \\(C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Retain arches with terminal gap in \\([h,2h]\\) on one chosen side and diameter at most \\(Ch\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Give each retained arch its critical weight and normalize over this entire set of terminal ports.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every integer \\(h\\to\\infty\\),\n\\[\nL(\\gamma)=h^{4/3+o(1)}\n\\quad\\text{in probability},\\qquad\n\\mathbb E L(\\gamma)=h^{4/3+o(1)}.\n\\]</p><p>This law sums over terminal ports before normalization and is distinct from the individual-gap law of Theorem 3.</p>", "proof": "<p>For sufficiently large fixed \\(C\\), Proposition 13's confined pointwise arch lower bound, summed over order-\\(h\\) gaps in \\([h,2h]\\), gives normalizer at least \\(ch^{-1/4}\\).</p><p>Translate the source to order-\\(h\\) distinct ports on the same straight side. All translated confined arches fit in one convex pure box of diameter \\(O_C(h)\\). Distinct sources distinguish outputs. Divide the calibrated convex-domain length bound \\(O_C(h^{25/12})\\) by the number of sources to get first length mass at most\n\\[\nC_Ch^{13/12}.\n\\]\nAfter normalization,\n\\[\n\\mathbb EL\\le C_Ch^{4/3},\n\\]\nand Markov gives the upper probability tail.</p><p>Every retained arch has diameter at least a fixed multiple of \\(h\\). Proposition 13(iii), summed over the finitely many diameter bins through \\(Ch\\), makes short-length mass superpolynomially small. This proves the lower probability tail and the mean lower bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 5", "orig": "Theorem 10.1 (One set of density-one heights)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is one set \\(G\\subseteq\\mathbb N\\) with \\( \\frac{|G\\cap[1,N]|}{N}\\longrightarrow1 \\) such that, for every fixed \\(\\delta,\\varepsilon>0\\), all sufficiently large \\(h\\in G\\) satisfy …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal B_h(e)\\) be the strict bridges from bottom coordinate \\(0\\) to top coordinate \\(e\\in\\mathbb Z+h/2\\) at integral height \\(h\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  b_h(e)=\\sum_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal B_h(e)}\\kappa^{L(\\gamma)},\n  \\qquad\n  b_h^{(L)}(e)=\n  \\sum_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal B_h(e)}L(\\gamma)\\kappa^{L(\\gamma)}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Thus \\(b_h=\\sum_e b_h(e)=B_h\\), and \\(b_0(e)=\\mathbf1_{\\{e=0\\}}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is one set \\(G\\subseteq\\mathbb N\\) with\n\\[\n\\frac{|G\\cap[1,N]|}{N}\\longrightarrow1\n\\]\nsuch that, for every fixed \\(\\delta,\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), all sufficiently large \\(h\\in G\\) satisfy\n\\[\nh^{4/3-\\varepsilon}\n\\le \\frac{b_h^{(L)}(e)}{b_h(e)}\n\\le h^{4/3+\\varepsilon}\n\\]\nuniformly for\n\\[\ne\\in\\mathbb Z+h/2,\\qquad |e|\\le h^{1-\\delta}.\n\\]\nThe set \\(G\\) is independent of \\(\\delta\\) and \\(\\varepsilon\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nU_h=\\sup_e b_h(e),\\qquad\nV_h=\\sup_e b_h^{(L)}(e),\n\\]\non actual row cosets. Propositions 18 and 20 give, on dyadic intervals,\n\\[\n\\sum_{H\\le h&lt;2H}U_h\\le H^{-\\alpha+o(1)},\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{H\\le h&lt;2H}V_h\\le H^{d_*+\\beta-1+o(1)}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\rho_H\\to\\infty\\), \\(\\rho_H=H^{o(1)}\\), so both right sides are bounded by the corresponding powers times \\(\\rho_H\\).</p><p>Retain in each dyadic interval precisely those heights satisfying\n\\[\nU_h\\le\\rho_H^2H^{-1-\\alpha},\n\\qquad\nV_h\\le\\rho_H^2H^{d_*+\\beta-2},\n\\]\nand let \\(G\\) be their union. At most \\(2H/\\rho_H=o(H)\\) heights are removed per interval. Summing over dyadic intervals gives density one.</p><p>On \\(G\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_e b_h(e)\\le h^{-5/4+o(1)},\\qquad\n\\sup_e b_h^{(L)}(e)\\le h^{1/12+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThis construction uses global endpoint suprema and is independent of \\(\\delta,\\epsilon\\).</p><p>For each fixed \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), Propositions 19–20 supply, at every large height and uniformly in its admissible window,\n\\[\nb_h(e)\\ge h^{-5/4-o(1)},\\qquad\nb_h^{(L)}(e)\\ge h^{1/12-o(1)}.\n\\]\nCombine lower numerator with upper denominator and upper numerator with lower denominator:\n\\[\nh^{4/3-o(1)}\n\\le\\frac{b_h^{(L)}(e)}{b_h(e)}\n\\le h^{4/3+o(1)}\n\\qquad(h\\in G,\\ |e|\\le h^{1-\\delta}).\n\\]\nThis gives the stated quantifiers.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 6", "orig": "Theorem 10.2 (All-even aligned height mixture)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "As \\(H\\to\\infty\\) through all real scales, \\( Z_H=H^{-1/4+o(1)},\\qquad M_H=H^{13/12+o(1)},\\qquad \\mathbb E_H L=\\frac{M_H}{Z_H}=H^{4/3+o(1)}. \\) The height marginal is \\(b_h(0)/Z_H\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the bridge arrays of Theorem 5.</p><p class=\"li\">For real \\(H&gt;0\\), put\n  \\[\n  E_H=2\\mathbb N\\cap[H,2H],\\qquad\n  Z_H=\\sum_{h\\in E_H}b_h(0),\\qquad\n  M_H=\\sum_{h\\in E_H}b_h^{(L)}(0).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Give each pair \\((h,\\gamma)\\), with \\(h\\in E_H\\) and \\(\\gamma\\in\\mathcal B_h(0)\\), probability\n  \\[\n  \\frac{\\kappa^{L(\\gamma)}}{Z_H}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>As \\(H\\to\\infty\\) through all real scales,\n\\[\nZ_H=H^{-1/4+o(1)},\\qquad\nM_H=H^{13/12+o(1)},\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_H L=\\frac{M_H}{Z_H}=H^{4/3+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThe height marginal is \\(b_h(0)/Z_H\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the estimates before deleting exceptional heights in Theorem 5's proof.</p><p>At every sufficiently large even height, Propositions 19–20 give\n\\[\nb_h(0)\\ge h^{-5/4-o(1)},\\qquad\nb_h^{(L)}(0)\\ge h^{1/12-o(1)}.\n\\]\nThere are order \\(H\\) even heights in \\([H,2H]\\), so\n\\[\nZ_H\\ge H^{-1/4-o(1)},\\qquad\nM_H\\ge H^{13/12-o(1)}.\n\\]</p><p>The height-summed endpoint-supremum bounds of Propositions 18 and 20 give the reverse inequalities\n\\[\nZ_H\\le H^{-1/4+o(1)},\\qquad\nM_H\\le H^{13/12+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThus their ratio is \\(H^{4/3+o(1)}\\) at every large real scale. Normalization directly gives height marginal \\(b_h(0)/Z_H\\). No upper estimate at an individually discarded height is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Proposition 2.1 (Finite boundary and length inputs)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every integer \\(h\\ge0\\), \\( B_h\\asymp(1+h)^{-1/4}. \\) For all sufficiently large integers \\(h\\), the length-weighted bridge mass is at most \\(Ch^{13/12}\\), and \\( B_h\\{L\\ge ch^{4/3}\\}\\ge ch^{-1/4}. \\) For every …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the critical honeycomb port convention.</p><p class=\"li\">A strip of integral height \\(h\\) has physical thickness \\(dh\\), where \\(d=\\sqrt3/2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B_h\\) be its critical strict-bridge mass from one fixed bottom port, summed over compatible top ports; set \\(B_0=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A strict bridge stays between its boundary cuts. Its terminal offset lies on \\(\\mathbb Z+h/2\\) in horizontal side units. There is no translation factor in \\(B_h\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. For every integer \\(h\\ge0\\),\n   \\[\n   B_h\\asymp(1+h)^{-1/4}.\n   \\]\n   For all sufficiently large integers \\(h\\), the length-weighted bridge mass is at most \\(Ch^{13/12}\\), and\n   \\[\n   B_h\\{L\\ge ch^{4/3}\\}\\ge ch^{-1/4}.\n   \\]\n   For every integer \\(h\\ge1\\) and every \\(r\\ge0\\), bridges of diameter exceeding \\(r\\) have mass at most \\(C(1+r)^{-1/4}\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. In a convex pure domain, completed paths from one boundary source with diameter at least \\(r\\) have mass at most \\(C(1+r)^{-1/4}\\). For the half-plane arch kernel,\n   \\[\n   K_l\\asymp l^{-5/4},\\qquad K_{l+1}\\le K_l\n   \\quad(l\\ge1).\n   \\]\n   The same adjacent-port monotonicity comparison holds along a straight side of a convex pure truncation when both compared ports remain on that side. Arches of diameter at most \\(Al\\) retain mass \\(cl^{-5/4}\\) for a fixed \\(A\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. From a prescribed source \\(a\\) with inward pure normal \\(n\\), consider the terminal line\n   \\[\n   m\\cdot(z-a)=s,\n   \\]\n   where \\(m\\) is its outward pure unit normal, the angle between \\(n\\) and \\(m\\) is at most \\(\\pi/3\\), and \\(s&gt;0\\). Crossings staying in the initial half-plane and before the terminal line until their final exit have mass comparable to \\(s^{-1/4}\\). For each fixed positive relative tolerance and all sufficiently large admissible \\(s\\), the lower bound can be confined within that tolerance times \\(s\\) of the segment \\(a+[0,s]m\\). The two-cut upper bound holds without confinement.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. In a convex pure domain of diameter \\(O(R)\\), the total length-weighted chord mass summed over ordered boundary endpoint pairs is at most\n   \\[\n   R^{25/12+o(1)}.\n   \\]</p>", "proof": "<p>These are the finite results of the paper's reference [16, Theorem 1.1, Propositions 7.1, 7.4, 10.1, and Corollary 8.1]. Their analytic and finite geometric proofs are inputs from that companion. In particular, the free-boundary mean is a finite input, not a consequence of the prescribed-endpoint theorem. Reference [13, Theorem 1.1] supplies an independent fixed-corridor first moment.</p><p>The local contour identity used below has phase \\(e^{i\\sigma W}\\), with \\(\\sigma=3/8\\) and \\(W\\) the total turning. It is the usual observable phase \\(e^{-5iW/8}\\) multiplied by the terminal tangent relative to the initial direction; see [3, Section 2, Lemma 1]. At an unvisited center the two outgoing weights sum to the incoming weight because\n\\[\n2\\kappa\\cos(\\pi/8)=1.\n\\]\nPrefixes returning to a visited center cancel in pairs under reversal of their unfinished simple loop: their turn increments differ by \\(8\\pi/3\\), so their phases have opposite signs.</p><p>Summing in a finite domain gives signed exit mass one. In a convex domain the real exit coefficients lie in \\([\\cos(3\\pi/8),1]\\). Subtracting the identity for a convex subdomain bounds the mass lost on restriction by a fixed multiple of the mass to newly exposed sides. The diameter bound justifies exhaustion. The additional artificial-arc and spectator constructions are given in the later components where they are used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 2.2 (Renewal inputs)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The mass \\(p\\) is a probability, and successive complete pieces have independent law \\(p\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">An irreducible strict bridge has positive integral height and no intermediate singly crossed level.</p><p class=\"li\">Every strict bridge has a unique ordered factorization into irreducible pieces.</p><p class=\"li\">On irreducibles from a fixed port, put \\(p(\\gamma)=\\kappa^{L(\\gamma)}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Under \\(p\\), let \\(H,Z,L,D\\) denote one piece's height, transverse displacement, length, and diameter.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The mass \\(p\\) is a probability, and successive complete pieces have independent law \\(p\\). For \\(s&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{h\\ge0}B_he^{-sh}\n=(1-\\mathbb E_p e^{-sH})^{-1}\n\\asymp s^{-3/4}\n\\quad(s\\downarrow0).\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\np(H&gt;x)+p(D&gt;x)\\le Cx^{-3/4},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_p[L;H\\le x]\\le x^{7/12+o(1)},\n\\]\n\\[\np(L&gt;n)\\le n^{-9/16+o(1)},\n\\qquad\n1-\\mathbb E_p e^{-uL}=u^{9/16+o(1)}.\n\\]\nAlmost surely, for independent pieces, their first \\(k\\) heights and lengths sum respectively to\n\\[\nk^{4/3+o(1)},\\qquad k^{16/9+o(1)},\n\\]\nand their diameters sum to at most \\(k^{4/3+o(1)}\\).</p><p>Only whole pieces are independent; their coordinates are not asserted to be independent. The quantity \\(B_h\\) is a renewal occurrence mass, not a probability conditioned on a future height. No estimate here conditions a piece on its length.</p>", "proof": "<p>The honeycomb mid-edge irreducible construction is [2, Appendix]. The stated joint-law conventions and estimates are [15, Propositions 2.2 and 2.4, Remark 2.5, and Lemma 2.6]. These are the renewal inputs used in the paper.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 3.1 (Interior connectors)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The mass of connecting paths in the stated domain is at least \\( cR^{-5/4}. \\) Constants are uniform on fixed compact sets of scaled endpoint configurations.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use critical honeycomb chords between prescribed ports.</p><p class=\"li\">Consider either:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. two macroscopically separated side-interior ports in the hexagons of Theorem 1;</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. one prescribed port on each opposite cut of a parallel strip of thickness comparable to \\(R\\), with displacement \\(O(R)\\);</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. one prescribed port on each ray of a wedge in the compact nondegenerate geometries of Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The mass of connecting paths in the stated domain is at least\n\\[\ncR^{-5/4}.\n\\]\nConstants are uniform on fixed compact sets of scaled endpoint configurations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Adapt the cut-averaging construction of [16, Proposition 10.2], placing the routes in the interior.</p><p>First work in a hexagon and scale distances by \\(R\\). Start with short inward-normal segments from the two ports. Continue by disjoint simple interior arcs to two parallel lanes in a free interior ball, preserving cyclic order. The lanes approach a common pure transverse line from the same side. Their small fixed positive separation permits a completing arch of diameter at most a fixed multiple of that separation in the free half-ball beyond the line. Keep that half-ball disjoint from nonincident route portions.</p><p>Approximate the arcs by finitely many pure-normal segments, retaining initial and terminal straight portions and making consecutive direction changes at most \\(\\pi/3\\). Alternate adjacent pure directions spanning a smooth tangent, with a short step in their common direction at sector transitions. A sufficiently fine fixed mesh preserves nonlocal separation; positive projection in a common direction preserves simplicity locally. Each segment has length a fixed positive multiple of \\(R\\).</p><p>For a branch with guiding directions \\(n_j\\), give the cut at the end of segment \\(j\\) normal \\(n_j\\), the arriving direction. The next leg begins with inward normal \\(n_j\\) and travels to a cut with outward normal \\(n_{j+1}\\). Proposition 1(3) keeps the incoming leg before the junction cut and the outgoing leg after it. Confine legs to narrow corridors. Each junction has a small crossing neighborhood avoided by all nonincident portions; those portions need not avoid the entire infinite cut. All choices have positive clearances and persist under small changes of scaled endpoint data. Compactness makes their number and relative tolerances uniform.</p><p>Vary every junction cut in an admissible level window of width comparable to \\(R\\). Choose windows preserving the corridors and stage orders. The two branches share their terminal level; all other levels vary independently. If there are \\(k\\) legs altogether, the set \\(\\Lambda_R\\) of lists consists of \\(k-2\\) ordinary windows and one shared terminal window. Give it uniform product probability.</p><p>Let \\(I_\\lambda(\\gamma)\\) indicate that the chord splits at list \\(\\lambda\\) into the confined legs and completing arch. The splitting is unique. Put\n\\[\nF_R(\\gamma)=\\frac1{|\\Lambda_R|}\n \\sum_{\\lambda\\in\\Lambda_R}I_\\lambda(\\gamma),\n\\qquad\n\\int G\\,d\\mu=\\sum_{\\gamma:a\\to b}\\kappa^{L(\\gamma)}G(\\gamma).\n\\]\nMultiplying the \\(k\\) point-to-line lower bounds and the confined arch lower bound gives\n\\[\n\\int F_R\\,d\\mu\\ge cR^{-k/4-5/4}.\n\\]</p><p>For two lists, split each branch at both crossings in every junction neighborhood. The crossings occur in normal-level order: the incoming portions start below both levels, and outgoing portions end above both. Their intervening portion is a strict bridge of the level difference. There remain \\(k\\) long portions of separation comparable to \\(R\\), each costing \\(O(R^{-1/4})\\), and a terminal arch beyond the later shared line costing \\(O(R^{-5/4})\\).</p><p>For independent uniform \\(u,u'\\) in an order-\\(R\\) window,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E(1+|u-u'|)^{-\\alpha}\n\\le C_\\alpha R^{-\\alpha},\n\\qquad 0&lt;\\alpha&lt;1.\n\\]\nEach ordinary junction uses \\(\\alpha=1/4\\). The shared terminal level supplies two difference bridges and uses \\(\\alpha=1/2\\). Ties use \\(B_0=1\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\int F_R^2\\,d\\mu\n\\le C R^{-k/4}R^{-(k-2)/4}R^{-1/2}R^{-5/4}\n=CR^{-2k/4-5/4}.\n\\]\nCauchy–Schwarz yields\n\\[\n\\mu(F_R&gt;0)\\ge\n\\frac{(\\int F_R\\,d\\mu)^2}{\\int F_R^2\\,d\\mu}\n\\ge cR^{-5/4}.\n\\]</p><p>For a wedge, add distant pure cuts at distance \\(AR\\), with fixed sufficiently large \\(A\\). The endpoint clearances permit initial inward segments of length \\(cR\\), and the bounded convex patch contains an interior ball of radius \\(c'R\\) accessible by the separated routes. A finite compactness cover makes the choices uniform. The paths stay in the full wedge. The same bounded-patch construction applies to a parallel strip.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Under normalized critical weights on height-\\(h\\) strict bridges with fixed initial port and terminal port summed, \\( \\Pr\\{h^{4/3-\\delta}\\le L\\le h^{4/3+\\delta}\\}\\longrightarrow1 \\) for every \\(\\delta>0\\).", "statement": "<p>Bridge length and inward-displacement estimates.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the strict-bridge mass \\(B_h\\) and irreducible probability \\(p\\) of Propositions 1–2.</p><p class=\"li\">Heights are integral row heights; displacements and diameters use physical distances.</p><p class=\"li\">In the second assertion, \\(n,m\\) are pure unit normals with \\(m\\ne-n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. Under normalized critical weights on height-\\(h\\) strict bridges with fixed initial port and terminal port summed,\n   \\[\n   \\Pr\\{h^{4/3-\\delta}\\le L\\le h^{4/3+\\delta}\\}\\longrightarrow1\n   \\]\n   for every \\(\\delta&gt;0\\). The conclusion persists under any restriction retaining a fixed positive fraction of \\(B_h\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. There exist \\(b,B,A,c_0&gt;0\\), depending on \\(n,m\\) but not on \\(\\delta\\), such that, for every fixed \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) and all sufficiently large \\(h\\), strict \\(n\\)-bridges from a fixed port satisfying\n   \\[\n   bh\\le m\\cdot v\\le Bh,\\qquad D\\le Ah,\\qquad\n   h^{4/3-\\delta}\\le L\\le h^{4/3+\\delta}\n   \\]\n   have mass at least \\(c_0h^{-1/4}\\), where \\(v\\) is endpoint displacement. The threshold may depend on \\(\\delta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 proves the fixed-height assertion and its retained-fraction form. Lemma 4.2 proves the simultaneous displacement, diameter, and length window with constants independent of the fixed length slack.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.3 (Fixed-height bridge length)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the first setting of Proposition 4, the stated fixed-height length concentration holds, including after restrictions retaining a fixed positive fraction of \\(B_h\\).", "statement": "<p>Under the first setting of Proposition 4, the stated fixed-height length concentration holds, including after restrictions retaining a fixed positive fraction of \\(B_h\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>It suffices to take \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1\\). Proposition 1 gives\n\\[\nB_h^{-1}\\sum Lw\\le Ch^{4/3},\n\\]\nso Markov's inequality gives the upper tail.</p><p>For the lower tail, first obtain fixed \\(M,A&gt;1\\) such that\n\\[\np\\{x\\le H\\le Mx,\\ D\\le Ax\\}\\ge cx^{-3/4}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\nIndeed,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_p[1-e^{-H/(Kx)}]\\asymp(Kx)^{-3/4},\n\\]\nwhereas the contributions from \\(H\\le x\\) and \\(H&gt;Mx\\) are respectively \\(O(x^{-3/4}/K)\\) and \\(O((Mx)^{-3/4})\\). Choose first \\(K\\), then \\(M\\), large; finally use the diameter tail to choose \\(A\\ge M\\).</p><p>The window implies\n\\[\n\\sup_j\\Pr(H_1+\\cdots+H_k=j)\\le Ck^{-4/3}. \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nSymmetrize using an independent bounded copy of \\(H\\). With \\(x\\) a sufficiently small fixed multiple of \\(|\\theta|^{-1}\\), the window gives a cosine deficit and hence\n\\[\n1-|\\mathbb E e^{i\\theta H}|^2\\ge c|\\theta|^{3/4}\n\\]\nnear zero. There are finitely many modulus-one peaks on the actual lattice torus, the same estimate repeats near each, and the modulus is bounded away from one elsewhere. Fourier inversion proves (3.2).</p><p>For \\(k\\ge2\\), split into comparable independent halves. If total height is \\(h\\), one half reaches at least \\(h/2\\), at cost \\(Ckh^{-3/4}\\) by tail and truncated first moment. The other matches an atom at cost \\(Ck^{-4/3}\\). For \\(k=1\\), use \\(Ch^{-3/4}\\). Thus, for fixed \\(\\eta&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{1\\le k\\le h^{3/4-\\eta}}\n \\Pr\\Big\\{\\sum_{j\\le k}H_j=h\\Big\\}\n\\le Ch^{-3/4}\n \\left(1+\\sum_{2\\le k\\le h^{3/4-\\eta}}k^{-1/3}\\right)\n=o(B_h).\n\\]</p><p>For remaining counts, put \\(t=h^{4/3-\\delta}\\). Exponential Markov gives\n\\[\n\\Pr\\Big\\{\\sum_{j\\le k}L_j&lt;t\\Big\\}\n\\le e(\\mathbb E_p e^{-L/t})^k\n\\le e\\exp\\{-kt^{-9/16+o(1)}\\}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\eta&lt;9\\delta/16\\). Summing over \\(k&gt;h^{3/4-\\eta}\\) is stretched-exponentially small. Renewal factorization and division by \\(B_h\\) prove the lower tail. Division by any fixed positive fraction of \\(B_h\\) preserves both negligible exceptional masses.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.4 (Positive projections at two supports)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the second setting of Proposition 4, its inward-displacement mass bound holds.", "statement": "<p>Under the second setting of Proposition 4, its inward-displacement mass bound holds.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(m\\cdot n&gt;0\\), use a sufficiently narrow straight channel along \\(n\\).</p><p>The remaining angle is \\(2\\pi/3\\). Let \\(n'\\) be the pure normal at angle \\(\\pi/3\\) to both \\(n,m\\), and put \\(T=dh\\). First travel to an \\(n'\\)-normal cut at distance near \\(3T/2\\), then in direction \\(n\\) to the top cut of the \\(n\\)-strip. Proposition 1(3) guides the first leg along \\(n'\\), despite initial inward normal \\(n\\). At the intermediate position both \\(n\\)- and \\(m\\)-projections are near \\(3T/4\\). The last leg advances about \\(T/4\\) along \\(n\\), leaving \\(m\\)-projection near \\(5T/8\\). Small fixed tolerances keep the two legs between strip boundaries, on opposite sides of their junction cut, and within diameter \\(O(h)\\).</p><p>Average the junction cut over an order-\\(h\\) window. For one cut, the product mass is at least \\(ch^{-1/2}\\). For two cuts, recovery in level order gives two long turned crossings and one difference bridge, so the averaged joint mass is at most \\(Ch^{-3/4}\\). Cauchy–Schwarz gives mass \\(ch^{-1/4}\\).</p><p>In either angular case this is a fixed fraction of \\(B_h\\). Apply Lemma 4.1 with slightly smaller exponent slack to remove atypical lengths without changing the lower constant for all sufficiently large \\(h\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemma 3.5 (Mixed displacement estimate)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every fixed \\(C_0<\\infty\\), there is \\(C<\\infty\\) such that \\( \\sum_{|z|\\le C_0s}D_n(z)(1+|m\\cdot z|)^{-1/4} \\le Cs^{1/2} \\qquad(s\\ge1). \\) Independent copies \\(Y_j\\) satisfy …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix pure normals \\(n,m\\) with \\(m\\ne-n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a lattice translation \\(z\\) with \\(n\\cdot z&gt;0\\), let \\(D_n(z)\\) be the critical mass of strict \\(n\\)-bridges between prescribed positions separated by \\(z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(n\\cdot z&lt;0\\), set \\(D_n(z)=D_n(-z)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At zero height, set \\(D_n(0)=1\\) and \\(D_n(z)=0\\) for nonzero \\(z\\) with \\(n\\cdot z=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Y\\) be the \\(m\\)-projection of one \\(n\\)-directed irreducible displacement.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(C_0&lt;\\infty\\), there is \\(C&lt;\\infty\\) such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{|z|\\le C_0s}D_n(z)(1+|m\\cdot z|)^{-1/4}\n\\le Cs^{1/2}\n\\qquad(s\\ge1). \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nIndependent copies \\(Y_j\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\sup_y\\Pr\\Big\\{\\sum_{j\\le k}Y_j=y\\Big\\}\\le Ck^{-4/3},\n\\qquad\n\\sup_w\\mathbb E\\Big(1+\\Big|w+\\sum_{j\\le k}Y_j\\Big|\\Big)^{-1/4}\n\\le Ck^{-1/3}. \\tag{3.4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The window (3.1) and lateral reflection symmetry imply, for fixed \\(B&gt;b&gt;0\\),\n\\[\np\\{bx\\le |Y|\\le Bx\\}\\ge cx^{-3/4}.\n\\]\nThe \\(m\\)-projections of reflected displacements sum to \\(2(m\\cdot n)dH\\). Since \\(m\\cdot n\\ne0\\), at least one has magnitude comparable to \\(H\\), while the diameter cutoff bounds both above. The symmetrization and Fourier argument of Lemma 4.1 gives the first estimate in (3.4). Sum that atom bound with weight \\((1+|y|)^{-1/4}\\) over \\(|y|\\le k^{4/3}\\), and use \\(k^{-1/3}\\) outside. This is uniform over translated lattices and shifts \\(w\\).</p><p>For (3.3), it suffices to sum over \\(n\\cdot z\\ge0\\). Use renewal factorization and split \\(k\\ge2\\) pieces into comparable halves. The displacement restriction forces total height at most \\(C_1s\\). Height damping bounds this event for the first half by\n\\[\nC\\exp\\{-ck/s^{3/4}\\}.\n\\]\nCondition on that half and apply the second estimate of (3.4) to the other half, shifted by the first projection. Hence the sum is at most\n\\[\nC+C\\sum_{k\\ge2}e^{-ck/s^{3/4}}k^{-1/3}\n\\le Cs^{1/2}.\n\\]\nThe constant covers one piece and zero-height matching. No coordinates within one piece have been made independent.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 3.6 (Attachment moments)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a fixed-source construction with side-1 outer landing exactly \\(a\\), side-2 outer landing free, translation count \\(N_s\\), and critical output measure \\(\\mu_a\\), such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal E\\) be an event of input chords from a fixed source \\(a\\) to a terminal interval at distance comparable to \\(R\\); let its critical mass be \\(E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n,m\\) be the inward support normals at the two ends, with \\(m\\ne-n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For distinct supports, assume order-\\(R\\) endpoint clearance from the other support. For identical supports, use their common half-plane.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose small fixed \\(c&gt;0\\) and a large scale \\(s\\le cR\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Translate an input chord by \\(v\\), with \\(|v|\\le Cs\\) and\n  \\[\n  g_1=n\\cdot v/d,\\qquad g_2=m\\cdot v/d\n  \\]\n  between fixed positive multiples of \\(s\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Attach height-\\(g_i\\) bridges from translated endpoints to their original supports, restricted to diameter \\(O(s)\\) and lengths in \\([s^{4/3-\\delta},s^{4/3+\\delta}]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The two branches lie in separated endpoint neighborhoods and avoid the translated chord by their supports.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a fixed-source construction with side-1 outer landing exactly \\(a\\), side-2 outer landing free, translation count \\(N_s\\), and critical output measure \\(\\mu_a\\), such that\n\\[\n\\int N_s\\,d\\mu_a\\ge cs^{1/2}E,\\qquad\n\\int N_s^2\\,d\\mu_a\\le CsR^{-1/4}. \\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nThis applies in the full two-cut domain without an input diameter bound.</p><p>If input chords lie in a convex pure domain of diameter \\(O(R)\\), there is also a construction at \\(s=cR\\) with both outer landings free, all outputs in one convex pure domain of diameter \\(O(R)\\), and\n\\[\n\\int N_R^{\\mathrm{free}}\\,d\\mu_{\\mathrm{free}}\n \\ge cR^{3/2}E,\\qquad\n\\int (N_R^{\\mathrm{free}})^2\\,d\\mu_{\\mathrm{free}}\n \\le CR^{7/4}. \\tag{3.6}\n\\]\nHere \\(\\mu_{\\mathrm{free}}\\) sums critical weights over ordered endpoint pairs. Output length is input length plus the two attached lengths. Constants are uniform on fixed compact endpoint geometries and independent of \\(\\delta\\); thresholds may depend on \\(\\delta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Reverse the side-1 bridge so that it starts at \\(a\\) and has displacement exactly \\(v\\). Let \\(V_s\\) contain all translations in ranges wide enough to include Proposition 4's positive-projection window. At each of order \\(s\\) heights, that proposition gives restricted mass \\(c_0s^{-1/4}\\) after displacement summation. Thus total side-1 mass over \\(v\\) is at least \\(cs^{3/4}\\). Side 2 has a free outer landing and supplies \\(cs^{-1/4}\\) for each \\(v\\).</p><p>Let \\(A_v(\\mathcal E)\\) be the outputs from translation \\(v\\), and put\n\\[\nN_s(\\Gamma)=\\sum_{v\\in V_s}\\mathbf1_{\\{\\Gamma\\in A_v(\\mathcal E)\\}}.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(v\\), crossings of translated supports recover the outer bridges and central translated chord. On identical supports, the two crossings are localized at the respective ends. Critical weights multiply because joining ports carry no weight. This proves the first moment in (3.5).</p><p>For two translations \\(v,v'\\), order branch crossings by each branch's inward-normal height. Orders may disagree at the two ends, but both side-1 crossings precede both side-2 crossings, by localization. Split into five portions: side-1 outer bridge, side-1 difference bridge, central chord, side-2 difference bridge, side-2 outer bridge.</p><p>The central portion lies between innermost crossings and stays inside both corresponding supports. For identical supports, other-end localized branches lie outside this central portion. Once translations are fixed its initial point is prescribed, its terminal point free, and separation is order \\(R\\). Its mass is at most \\(CR^{-1/4}\\).</p><p>Put \\(z=v'-v\\). Side-1 difference mass is \\(D_n(z)\\); side-2 difference mass is at most \\(C(1+|m\\cdot z|)^{-1/4}\\). A side-1 height tie forces \\(v=v'\\), agreeing with the zero-height convention. Side-2 outer mass is \\(Cs^{-1/4}\\). If \\(v_{\\mathrm{out}}\\) is the earlier side-1 translation, then\n\\[\n\\sum_{v_{\\mathrm{out}}\\in V_s}D_n(v_{\\mathrm{out}})\n\\le C\\sum_{h\\le Cs}B_h\\le Cs^{3/4}.\n\\]\nDrop avoidance between the five portions and use Proposition 5 to sum difference factors. The accounting is\n\\[\n\\int N_s^2\\,d\\mu_a\n\\le C s^{3/4}s^{1/2}R^{-1/4}s^{-1/4}\n=CsR^{-1/4}.\n\\]\nOnly supports and endpoint clearance were used, not an input diameter bound.</p><p>For both free landings, sum over order \\(R^2\\) translations in a fixed positive sector, at \\(s=cR\\). Both outer bridges have a free endpoint, giving first moment \\(cR^2R^{-1/2}E\\). In the second moment, the side-1 outer sum becomes \\(O(R^2R^{-1/4})\\); the other factors are unchanged. Hence\n\\[\n\\int (N_R^{\\mathrm{free}})^2\\,d\\mu_{\\mathrm{free}}\n\\le CR^{2-1/4}R^{1/2}R^{-1/4}R^{-1/4}\n=CR^{7/4}.\n\\]\nThe localized attachments and \\(O(R)\\) translations of bounded input chords fit in one convex pure truncation of diameter \\(O(R)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Theorem 4.1 (Same-side probability law)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), \\( \\Pr\\{l^{4/3-\\varepsilon}\\le L(\\gamma)\\le l^{4/3+\\varepsilon}\\} \\longrightarrow1. \\) The same conclusion holds in the hexagons of Theorem 1 when the two scaled endpoints tend to distinct …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K_l\\) be the half-plane arch mass at prescribed integer separation \\(l\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize each arch by \\(\\kappa^{L(\\gamma)}/K_l\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr\\{l^{4/3-\\varepsilon}\\le L(\\gamma)\\le l^{4/3+\\varepsilon}\\}\n\\longrightarrow1.\n\\]\nThe same conclusion holds in the hexagons of Theorem 1 when the two scaled endpoints tend to distinct points in one side interior.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take \\(m=\\lfloor l^{1/2-\\eta}\\rfloor\\) for small fixed \\(\\eta&gt;0\\). By Lemmas 7.1–7.2, the good construction exhausts \\(1-o(1)\\) of the exact arch mass: the diameter sum is \\(o(l)\\) in probability, and the joining factor divided by \\(K_l\\) tends to one on good prefixes and is bounded there.</p><p>The pair-process height after \\(m\\) blocks is \\(m^{2+o(1)}\\) almost surely, by its height transform and the power-sum argument of Proposition 2. Along the corresponding renewal subsequences, each individual cumulative length is\n\\[\n(m^2)^{4/3+o(1)}.\n\\]\nTaking \\(\\eta\\) small makes the prefix exponents arbitrarily close to \\(4/3\\) in probability. Bounded reweighting transfers these estimates to good constructed paths.</p><p>For a joining arch longer than \\(\\frac12l^{4/3+\\epsilon}\\), the lateral atom estimate, after translating its first endpoint to a fixed origin, bounds mass by\n\\[\nCm^{-2}\n \\left(CR^{-1/4}\n       +l^{-4/3-\\epsilon}R^{13/12+o(1)}\\right),\n\\qquad R=l^{1+\\gamma}.\n\\]\nThe first term uses the half-plane diameter tail beyond \\(R\\). For smaller diameter, translate the fixed source to order \\(R\\) equivalent starts along one base side of a common size-\\(O(R)\\) convex truncation. Proposition 1(4), divided by that number of starts, bounds the summed length mass by \\(R^{13/12+o(1)}\\).</p><p>Choose \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\) small relative to \\(\\epsilon\\), then \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) small enough that\n\\[\n2\\eta&lt;\\gamma/4,\\qquad\n2\\eta+13\\gamma/12&lt;\\epsilon.\n\\]\nBoth contributions are \\(o(l^{-5/4})\\). This proves half-plane concentration.</p><p>For the same-side hexagon law, \\(l\\asymp R\\). Proposition 3 gives normalizer at least \\(cl^{-5/4}\\), while its exceptional mass is bounded by the full half-plane exceptional mass \\(o(l^{-5/4})\\). Divide by the finite-domain normalizer.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.2 (Common-block estimates)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The mass \\(r\\) is a probability, successive pair blocks regenerate independently, and \\( r(T>x)+r(D_*>x)\\le Cx^{-1/2}, \\qquad \\sup_j\\Pr(Y^{(m)}=j)\\le Cm^{-2}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Run two independent upward irreducible sequences of law \\(p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Stop at their next common positive renewal height.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(r\\) be the mass on finite terminating pairs, initially possibly a subprobability, and let \\(T\\) be their common height.</p><p class=\"li\">A pair block contains the complete bridge segment from each sequence. Let \\(D_*\\) be the larger segment diameter, and \\(Y\\) the second lateral displacement minus the first. Thus \\(|Y|\\le2D_*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Y^{(m)}\\) be the sum of lateral differences of \\(m\\) independent pair blocks.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The mass \\(r\\) is a probability, successive pair blocks regenerate independently, and\n\\[\nr(T&gt;x)+r(D_*&gt;x)\\le Cx^{-1/2},\n\\qquad\n\\sup_j\\Pr(Y^{(m)}=j)\\le Cm^{-2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The common renewal masses are \\(B_h^2\\), so\n\\[\nC(s):=\\sum_{h\\ge0}e^{-sh}B_h^2\n=(1-\\widehat r(s))^{-1}\\asymp s^{-1/2},\n\\qquad\n\\widehat r(s)=\\sum re^{-sT}.\n\\]\nSending \\(s\\downarrow0\\) gives total mass one, and\n\\(1-\\widehat r(s)\\asymp s^{1/2}\\) gives the height tail. Regeneration follows because testing a terminating pair uses only pieces up to its termination.</p><p>For the diameter tail, use \\(s=D/x\\) with fixed large \\(D\\). By the ordinary bridge diameter bound on one total bridge and the \\(B_h\\) bound on the other, the normalized fraction of terminated lists containing a block with \\(D_*&gt;x\\) is at most\n\\[\nCx^{-1/4}s^{-3/4}/C(s)&lt;1/2.\n\\]\nWriting \\(d_s=1-\\widehat r(s)\\) and\n\\(q_s=\\mathbb E_r[e^{-sT};D_*&gt;x]\\), that fraction is\n\\(q_s/(d_s+q_s)\\). Hence \\(q_s\\le d_s=O(x^{-1/2})\\). Remove discount below height \\(x\\) and use the height tail above it.</p><p>Next establish fixed \\(D_1&gt;D_0&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\nr(D_0x\\le |Y|\\le D_1x)\\ge cx^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nAt each large height \\(h\\), ordinary bridges have mass \\(\\gtrsim h^{-1/4}\\) with lateral displacement between positive fixed multiples of \\(h\\). To construct them, travel in a direction inclined \\(60^\\circ\\) to vertical to a tilted normal cut near distance \\((3/2)dh\\), then vertically to the top. Proposition 1(3), with small tolerances, keeps the first leg below the top, the second above the base, and the legs on opposite sides of their joining cut. Averaging over an order-\\(h\\) tilted-level window gives first moment \\(ch^{-1/2}\\). For two levels \\(t,t'\\), recovery gives joint mass\n\\[\nCh^{-1/2}(1+|t-t'|)^{-1/4}.\n\\]\nIts average is \\(O(h^{-3/4})\\), so Cauchy–Schwarz gives the asserted off-axis mass.</p><p>Normalize \\(C(1/x)\\) to probability on terminated sequences. In \\(x\\le h\\le2x\\), apply opposite off-axis restrictions to retain positive probability of total absolute difference at least \\(ax\\). The number of pair blocks is geometric with mean \\(O(x^{1/2})\\); conditional block laws are tilted by \\(e^{-T/x}/\\widehat r(1/x)\\). The diameter tail bounds the probability of a term larger than \\(D_1x\\) by \\(O(D_1^{-1/2})\\), while the expected absolute sum of terms below \\(D_0x\\) is \\(O(\\sqrt{D_0}\\,x)\\). Choose \\(D_1\\) large and \\(D_0\\) small. A window term occurs with positive probability, and the mean-count bound proves the claim under \\(r\\).</p><p>The law of \\(Y\\) is symmetric and integer-valued, since equal-height endpoints have the same row stagger. Symmetrization with an independent bounded copy gives\n\\[\n1-|\\mathbb E e^{i\\theta Y}|^2\\gtrsim|\\theta|^{1/2}\n\\]\nnear zero. The finitely many torus peaks repeat this estimate. Fourier inversion gives \\(Cm^{-2}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.3 (Sublinear changes of the gap)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For positive integers \\(l,l'\\) with \\(l\\to\\infty\\) and \\(l'/l\\to1\\), \\( K_{l'}/K_l\\longrightarrow1. \\)", "statement": "<p>For positive integers \\(l,l'\\) with \\(l\\to\\infty\\) and \\(l'/l\\to1\\),\n\\[\nK_{l'}/K_l\\longrightarrow1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For starting separation \\(j\\), call \\(m\\)-block prefixes good when\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\le m}D_{*,i}&lt;j/4.\n\\]\nTheir failure probability is \\(O(m/\\sqrt j)\\), by truncation. On good prefixes the two arms are disjoint; join their endpoints above the attained common level by any half-plane arch. Thus\n\\[\nK_j\\ge\\mathbb E[\\mathbf1_{\\mathrm{good}}K_{j+Y^{(m)}}].\n\\]\nThis construction is injective: the first \\(m\\) positive base-parallel levels with exactly two crossings are precisely the sampled common renewals. Before the joining level, each branch crosses each level, and one crossing is exactly renewal within that branch. The joining level has the two prescribed crossings.</p><p>Use monotonicity and \\(K_l\\asymp l^{-5/4}\\). If the conclusion failed, there would be drops at least \\(\\epsilon l^{-5/4}\\) between \\(l-w\\) and \\(l+w\\), with \\(1\\le w=o(l)\\). For large integer \\(q\\), put \\(m=\\lfloor\\sqrt q\\rfloor\\). Lemma 7.1 and symmetry give\n\\[\n\\Pr(Y^{(m)}\\le-bq)\\ge1/4\n\\]\nfor small fixed \\(b&gt;0\\); the diameter tail and truncation give\n\\[\n\\Pr(|Y^{(m)}|&gt;Aq)=O(A^{-1/2}).\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(A\\) large. Choose \\(w\\ll q\\ll l\\) so variation on\n\\[\n[l+bq/4,\\ l+(A+b+1)q]\n\\]\nis \\(o(l^{-5/4})\\). Arbitrarily many disjoint such geometric bands exist between the two scales, and their variations sum to \\(O(l^{-5/4})\\).</p><p>At \\(j=l+\\lfloor bq/2\\rfloor\\), good prefixes with \\(|Y^{(m)}|\\le Aq\\) give joining factor at least \\(K_j-o(l^{-5/4})\\). Those also satisfying \\(Y^{(m)}\\le-bq\\) land below \\(l-w\\) and gain at least \\(\\epsilon l^{-5/4}\\). Good has probability tending to one because \\(m/\\sqrt j=O(\\sqrt{q/l})\\). The discarded tail loses \\(O(A^{-1/2})K_j\\), whereas the gain has probability at least \\(1/4-O(A^{-1/2})-o(1)\\). Taking \\(A\\) large in terms of \\(\\epsilon\\) contradicts the construction inequality.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 5.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every fixed integer \\(\\ell\\ge0\\) and every \\(\\eta>0\\), there is \\(C_{\\ell,\\eta}<\\infty\\) such that \\( |\\Delta^\\ell B_N| \\le C_{\\ell,\\eta}N^{-1/4-\\ell+\\eta} \\qquad(N\\ge1). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B_N\\) be the critical mass of strict bridges from one fixed port to all ports on the opposite pure cut at integral height \\(N\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\Delta f_N=f_{N+1}-f_N\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed integer \\(\\ell\\ge0\\) and every \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), there is \\(C_{\\ell,\\eta}&lt;\\infty\\) such that\n\\[\n|\\Delta^\\ell B_N|\n\\le C_{\\ell,\\eta}N^{-1/4-\\ell+\\eta}\n\\qquad(N\\ge1).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use (5.2), justified for large \\(N\\) by Lemma 8.6. Lemma 8.5 and the discrete inverse rule give\n\\[\n\\Delta^\\ell\\!\\left(\\frac1{r_N(c_3-h_1)(0)}\\right)\n=N^{-\\ell+o(1)}\n\\]\nas an upper bound.</p><p>The term \\(r_Nh_0(-4)\\) has differences\n\\[\nN^{-1+2b/\\pi-\\ell+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThe numerator product\n\\[\nr_N(c_3-h_1)(-4)\\,r_Nh_0(0)\n\\]\nhas the same bound, by discrete product rules. Apply product and quotient rules in (5.2). Since \\(2b/\\pi=3/4\\),\n\\[\n|\\Delta^\\ell B_N|\n\\le N^{-1/4-\\ell+o(1)}.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(\\ell,\\eta\\), absorb finitely many remaining \\(N\\) into \\(C_{\\ell,\\eta}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The following finite facts are inputs from [16, Theorem 2.1 and Lemmas 3.1–3.4].", "statement": "<p>Finite strip representation and positive interpolation inputs; original location: Section 5.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Set\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\pi/8,\\qquad b=3\\lambda,\\qquad\nC_t(x)=\\cosh(t\\sqrt x),\\qquad D(x)=2C_\\lambda(x)-1,\n\\]\n\\[\na_*=D(-4)=2\\cos(2\\lambda)-1&gt;0,\n\\qquad\nd\\mu(x)=\\frac{zD(z^2)}{\\sinh(4\\lambda z)}\\,dz,\n\\quad x=z^2,\\ z&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor \\(N\\ge1\\), define\n\\[\nM_N=\\operatorname{span}\n \\{1,x,\\ldots,x^{N-1},\n   \\partial_t^jC_t|_{t=\\lambda}:0\\le j&lt;N\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nc_1=C_{\\lambda/2},\\quad c_3=C_{3\\lambda/2},\n\\quad h_0=D^{-1},\\quad h_1=c_1/D,\\quad\ng=\\int_0^\\lambda C_t\\,dt,\n\\]\n\\[\nF_{0,N}=M_N+\\mathbb Rc_1,\\qquad G_N=M_N+\\mathbb Rg.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\Pi_Nh\\in F_{0,N}\\) preserve all pairings against \\(G_N\\) in \\(L^2(\\mu)\\), and put \\(r_Nh=h-\\Pi_Nh\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The following finite facts are inputs from [16, Theorem 2.1 and Lemmas 3.1–3.4].</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The projection exists uniquely, also for \\(c_3\\), whose relevant pairings are finite although \\(c_3\\notin L^2(\\mu)\\). Whenever its denominator is nonzero,\n   \\[\n   B_N=2a_*\n   \\left[\n   r_Nh_0(-4)-\n   \\frac{r_N(c_3-h_1)(-4)\\,r_Nh_0(0)}\n        {r_N(c_3-h_1)(0)}\n   \\right]. \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n   Undivided, there is \\(s_N\\) such that\n   \\[\n   2B_N=4a_*[r_Nh_0(-4)+s_Nr_Nh_0(0)],\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   r_N(c_3-h_1)(-4)+s_Nr_N(c_3-h_1)(0)=0.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For \\(0&lt;t&lt;\\lambda\\), put \\(G_{N,t}=M_N+\\mathbb RC_t\\). On ordered positive nodes \\(x_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;x_{2N+1}\\), products of evaluation determinants for \\((F_{0,N},G_{N,t})\\), times positive base weights, give positive densities whenever moments are finite. Their mean interpolant is the oblique projection. The negative interpolation residual \\(-e_0c_3\\) at zero is positive and increasing in each ordered node. Multiplication of the base weight by an increasing positive function increases its expectation. Replacing \\(G_{N,t}\\) by \\(G_N\\) is a positive mixture.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Set\n   \\[\n   F_N=F_{0,N}+\\mathbb Rc_3,\\qquad\n   A_N=\\{f\\in F_N:f(0)=0\\},\\qquad\n   U_N=\\{f\\in M_N:f(0)=0\\}.\n   \\]\n   For the densities \\((F_{0,N},G_{N,t})\\) and \\((A_N,G_{N,t})\\), expectations of\n   \\[\n   \\prod_i r(x_i),\\qquad r(x)=\\frac{x+\\alpha}{x+\\beta},\n   \\quad \\alpha,\\beta&gt;0,\n   \\]\n   differ by bounded factors from the respective symmetric expectations for \\((M_N,M_N)\\) and \\((U_N,U_N)\\). Constants are uniform in \\(N,t\\) and persist under a fixed number of such rational weight changes. The two symmetric expectations also differ by a bounded factor.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. For the probability measure \\(\\mu_c\\) proportional to \\(dz/\\cosh(cz)\\), with \\(x=z^2\\), put \\(\\alpha_c=\\pi/(2c)\\) and \\(w_t=\\tan^2(\\alpha_ct)\\). Then\n   \\[\n   A_t(x)=\\cos(\\alpha_ct)C_t(x),\\qquad\n   \\int A_tA_s\\,d\\mu_c=\\frac1{1-w_tw_s}. \\tag{5.3}\n   \\]\n   Thus \\(A_t=\\sum_{j\\ge0}P_j(x)w_t^j\\) generates orthonormal polynomials of degree \\(j\\). The identity and its differentiated forms hold for \\(|t|+|s|&lt;c\\).</p><p>These inputs apply to \\(\\mu\\), \\(dW=z\\,dz/\\sinh(bz)\\), and fixed rational modifications \\(r^jW\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>These are the finite strip inputs stated in Section 5.1 and supplied by [16].</p><p>For the interlacing input, the positive-weight and exponential-domination hypotheses persist under each fixed rational change. The bound uses only the range of \\(\\log r\\), not \\(t\\). Interlacing of the positive roots of compressed multiplication forms gives the comparison; a codimension-one compression changes the logarithmic product by at most the range of \\(\\log r\\). All base weights used here have the required exponential domination.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The cosine transform identifies \\(h\\in M_N^\\perp\\subset L^2(\\mu)\\) with \\( \\int h(x)C_\\omega(x)\\,d\\mu(x)=T_NA(\\omega). \\) The corresponding transported norm \\(\\mathcal G_N\\), and the comparison norm …", "statement": "<p>Transport of the residual space; original location: Section 5.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Lemma 8.1's analytic setting. Put\n\\[\nv(\\omega)=\\tan\\frac{\\pi\\omega}{2b},\\qquad\nJ(\\omega)=v'(\\omega),\\qquad\nQ(v)=\\frac{v^2(v^2-v(\\lambda)^2)}\n           {1-v^2v(\\lambda)^2},\n\\]\nand, for even holomorphic \\(A\\) on the unit disk, define\n\\[\n(T_NA)(\\omega)=J(\\omega)Q(v(\\omega))^NA(v(\\omega)).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The cosine transform identifies \\(h\\in M_N^\\perp\\subset L^2(\\mu)\\) with\n\\[\n\\int h(x)C_\\omega(x)\\,d\\mu(x)=T_NA(\\omega). \\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nThe corresponding transported norm \\(\\mathcal G_N\\), and the comparison norm\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_N^0(A,A)\n=c_b\\int_{|v|&lt;1}|Q(v)|^{2N}|A(v)|^2\\,d^2v,\n\\qquad c_b&gt;0, \\tag{5.5}\n\\]\nare uniformly equivalent. For \\(A(v)=\\sum_{j\\ge0}a_jv^{2j}\\), both are equivalent to\n\\[\n\\|A\\|_N^2=\\sum_{j\\ge0}\\frac{|a_j|^2}{N+j}. \\tag{5.6}\n\\]\nEvery fixed \\(\\ell\\)-th difference of these forms, and of their inverse forms, costs \\(O_\\ell(N^{-\\ell})\\) relative to this norm.</p>", "proof": "<p>The map \\(v\\) sends \\(|\\operatorname{Re}\\omega|&lt;b/2\\) bijectively to the disk. The even finite Blaschke product \\(Q\\) has a double zero at zero and simple zeros at \\(\\pm v(\\lambda)\\). Orthogonality against \\(M_N\\) is exactly a transform zero of order \\(2N\\) at zero and zeros of order \\(N\\) at \\(\\pm\\lambda\\). Division by \\(JQ^N\\) gives even holomorphic \\(A\\), and conversely these zeros give orthogonality.</p><p>Let \\(w_\\mu,w_W\\) be the even extensions of the \\(z\\)-densities. With \\(f(z)=h(z^2)w_\\mu(z)\\),\n\\[\nF(\\omega)=\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R}f(z)e^{\\omega z}\\,dz,\n\\qquad\n\\|h\\|_{L^2(\\mu)}^2\n=\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\frac{|f(z)|^2}{w_\\mu(z)}\\,dz.\n\\]\nHold \\(F\\), hence \\(f\\), fixed. In measure \\(W\\), the representing function is \\(h_W=h\\,d\\mu/dW\\). Integrating Plancherel over the strip supplies reciprocal weight \\(\\sinh(bz)/z=1/w_W(z)\\). The conformal Jacobian gives (5.5). Conversely, Fourier transforming the Cauchy–Riemann equation locally in frequency forces the same exponential multiplier on parallel lines. Strip square integrability gives the weighted \\(L^2\\) datum, and division by the even weight recovers \\(h\\).</p><p>The ratio \\(d\\mu/dW\\) is bounded above and below. More precisely, the difference of reciprocal weights is, up to a fixed factor,\n\\[\nz^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{\\sinh(4\\lambda z)}{D(z^2)}-\\sinh(bz)\\right\\}.\n\\]\nIt is bounded at zero and \\(O(e^{2d_{\\rm tr}|z|})\\) at infinity for fixed \\(d_{\\rm tr}&lt;b/2\\) close enough to \\(b/2\\), because leading exponential terms cancel. Thus the norm correction is a fixed bounded sesquilinear form on traces \\(T_NA(\\pm d_{\\rm tr}+iy)\\).</p><p>The boundary phase speed of \\(Q\\) is smooth and strictly positive. Hence \\(|Q(re^{i\\theta})|^{2N}\\) is bounded above by \\(e^{-cN(1-r)}\\) and below near the boundary by \\(e^{-CN(1-r)}\\). Angular orthogonality and radial integration give (5.6).</p><p>Differencing \\(\\mathcal G_N^0\\) inserts \\((|Q|^2-1)^\\ell\\), giving \\(N^{-\\ell}\\). On either fixed interior trace, \\(v\\to\\pm i\\) nontangentially at its ends and \\(Q(\\pm i)=1\\), so\n\\[\n|1-Q|\\le C(1-|Q|).\n\\]\nA difference of \\(T_N\\) inserts \\(Q-1\\), and comparison with \\(T_{\\lfloor N/2\\rfloor}\\) costs \\(O(N^{-1})\\). Mean-value bounds control interior trace norms by strip area norms. The discrete product rule gives the same estimates for the correction form. Finitely shifted norms are equivalent. Differencing \\(\\mathcal G_N^{-1}\\mathcal G_N=I\\) gives the inverse-form estimates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.3", "orig": "Lemma 5.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On every compact subset of the closed bidisk where \\( |1-v^2\\overline u^{\\,2}|>0, \\) the kernel has continuous boundary values and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K_N^0(v,u)\\) be the evaluation kernel for \\(\\mathcal G_N^0\\), analytic in \\(v\\), anti-analytic in \\(u\\), with the inner product linear in its first argument.</p><p class=\"li\">Thus\n  \\[\n  A(u)=\\mathcal G_N^0(A,K_N^0(\\cdot,u)).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On every compact subset of the closed bidisk where\n\\[\n|1-v^2\\overline u^{\\,2}|&gt;0,\n\\]\nthe kernel has continuous boundary values and\n\\[\n|\\Delta^\\ell K_N^0(v,u)|\\le C_\\ell N^{1-\\ell},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{K_N^0(v,u)}N\n\\longrightarrow\n\\frac{O(v)\\overline{O(u)}}{1-v^2\\overline u^{\\,2}}.\n\\]\nHere \\(O\\) is even, holomorphic and nonvanishing on a neighborhood of the closed disk, and positive on both real and imaginary diameters.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(P_{jk}\\) be the Gram matrix of \\(v^{2j}\\) in (5.5), and \\(R=P^{-1}\\). In the open bidisk,\n\\[\nK_N^0(v,u)=\\sum_{j,k\\ge0}v^{2j}R_{jk}\\overline u^{\\,2k}.\n\\]\nWrite \\(\\delta\\) for simultaneous forward difference in \\(j,k\\). For fixed nonnegative \\(\\ell,s\\),\n\\[\n|\\Delta^\\ell\\delta^sP_{jk}|\n\\le C_{\\ell,s}e^{-c|j-k|}\n (N+j+k)^{-1-\\ell-s}. \\tag{5.7}\n\\]\nThe differences insert \\((|Q|^2-1)^\\ell(r^4-1)^s\\). Shift the angular Fourier contour by a small fixed imaginary amount in the decay direction. The continued product \\(Q(re^{i\\theta})Q(re^{-i\\theta})\\) equals one at \\(r=1\\); its positive radial logarithmic derivative remains positive under the shift, while compactness gives strict modulus below one away from the boundary. Radial integration proves (5.7).</p><p>The inverse satisfies\n\\[\n|\\Delta^\\ell\\delta^sR_{jk}|\n\\le C_{\\ell,s}e^{-c|j-k|}\n (N+j+k)N^{-\\ell}(1+\\min\\{j,k\\})^{-s}. \\tag{5.8}\n\\]\nFor \\(\\ell=s=0\\), normalize \\(P\\) by diagonal factors \\(\\sqrt{N+j}\\). By (5.6), the normalized positive operator has bounded positive inverse. Conjugation by weights with sufficiently small exponential logarithmic slope changes it by arbitrarily small operator norm, by row and column sums in (5.7). Invert, truncate the weights, and pass to the limit. This is the localized-inverse argument compared with [7, Proposition 2].</p><p>For differences, set \\((SA)_{jk}=A_{j+1,k+1}\\). The half-line product rule is\n\\[\nS(AB)=(SA)(SB)+E(A,B),\\qquad\nE(A,B)_{jk}=A_{j+1,0}B_{0,k+1}.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\delta R=-R(\\delta P)SR-RE(P,R),\\qquad\n\\Delta R_N=-R_N(\\Delta P_N)R_{N+1}.\n\\]\nExponentially localized matrix bounds compose by adding polynomial orders. Boundary terms decay exponentially in \\(j+k\\), paying every fixed index-decay factor. First establish all pure \\(N\\)-differences; then induct on \\(s\\) in the first identity, retaining every fixed \\(\\ell\\). The extra difference on \\(P\\) and boundary localization give (5.8).</p><p>For boundary convergence off the even diagonal, fix \\(j-k\\), put \\(n=\\min(j,k)\\), and use a smooth dyadic partition in \\(n\\). Summation by parts \\(s\\) times in the factor \\((v^2\\overline u^{\\,2})^n\\), followed by summation of exponentially decaying matrix diagonals, bounds the \\(n\\asymp m\\) block of \\(N^{\\ell-1}\\Delta^\\ell K_N^0\\) by \\(C_sm^{2-s}\\). For \\(s&gt;2\\), the dyadic sum converges uniformly on the stated compact sets and gives continuity and the difference bound.</p><p>For fixed \\(j,k\\), radial Laplace integration gives a positive Toeplitz limit for \\(NP_{jk}\\), with symbol a positive constant times reciprocal boundary phase speed, restricted to even powers. Exponential matrix decay permits passage through the inverse identities. Factor the symbol as \\(1/|O|^2\\) by harmonic extension of its logarithm and a harmonic conjugate, normalized by \\(O(0)&gt;0\\), as in [17, Section 5]. Real analyticity, evenness, and reflection give analytic continuation and positivity on both diameters. Multiplication by \\(1/O\\) identifies the weighted even Hardy space with the ordinary one, whose kernel is \\((1-v^2\\overline u^{\\,2})^{-1}\\). The same dyadic tail bound makes convergence uniform.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every fixed \\(\\ell\\), \\( \\|\\Delta^\\ell(K_N(\\cdot,u)-K_N^0(\\cdot,u))\\|_N \\le C_\\ell N^{-\\ell}. \\) At \\(u=1\\), the difference of columns has a finite-norm limit.", "statement": "<p>Change-of-measure kernel correction; original equation (5.9).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K_N\\) be the evaluation kernel for \\(\\mathcal G_N\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u=v(t)\\), with \\(0\\le t\\le\\lambda\\), or take \\(t\\uparrow b/2\\), so \\(u\\to1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(\\ell\\),\n\\[\n\\|\\Delta^\\ell(K_N(\\cdot,u)-K_N^0(\\cdot,u))\\|_N\n\\le C_\\ell N^{-\\ell}. \\tag{5.9}\n\\]\nAt \\(u=1\\), the difference of columns has a finite-norm limit. The full boundary column is defined as the model boundary column plus this correction; neither full boundary column is asserted to have finite norm.</p><p>For variables corresponding to \\(0\\le t\\le\\lambda\\), the actual kernel has the same strictly positive leading limit after division by \\(N\\), and differences \\(O(N^{1-\\ell})\\). This also holds when exactly one variable is \\(1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>A point on either fixed interior trace and any stated \\(u\\) remain uniformly off the even diagonal. On the traces,\n\\[\n|J|\\asymp e^{-\\pi|y|/b},\\qquad\n1-|Q|\\gtrsim e^{-\\pi|y|/b}.\n\\]\nLemma 8.3 and discrete product rules give\n\\[\n\\|\\Delta^\\ell(T_NK_N^0(\\cdot,u))\\|_\n {L^2(\\{\\pm d_{\\rm tr}+iy\\})}\n\\le C_\\ell N^{-\\ell}.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\ell=0\\), the integrand is bounded by\n\\[\nCN e^{-\\pi|y|/b}\n \\exp\\{-cNe^{-\\pi|y|/b}\\},\n\\]\nwhose squared integral is bounded. Each difference gives the additional stated factor.</p><p>Apply the bounded correction form of Lemma 8.2 to this model column and solve with \\(\\mathcal G_N^{-1}\\). The resolvent identity and inverse-form differences prove (5.9). Dominated convergence on traces gives the boundary interpretation. Evaluation at a fixed interior point costs \\(O(\\sqrt N)\\) in (5.6), so the finite-norm correction is lower order than the positive order-\\(N\\) model kernel.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Residual upper estimates; first two lines of original equation (5.10).", "statement": "<p>Residual upper estimates; first two lines of original equation (5.10).</p><p>Under Lemma 8.1's setting, for fixed \\(\\ell\\), \\(j=0,1\\), and \\(a=0,4\\),\n\\[\n|\\Delta^\\ell r_Nh_j(-a)|\n\\le N^{-1+b\\sqrt a/\\pi-\\ell+o(1)},\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\Delta^\\ell r_Nc_3(-a)|\n\\le N^{b\\sqrt a/\\pi-\\ell+o(1)}.\n\\]\nEach fixed positive exponent slack is allowed, with its own constant.</p>", "proof": "<p>First take the orthogonal residual against \\(M_N\\) alone. For \\(h=h_j\\), write its transform as \\(T_NA_{h,N}\\) and\n\\[\nh(z^2)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}f_h(y)\\cos(yz)\\,dy.\n\\]\nThe function \\(f_h\\) is integrable against \\(e^{u|y|}\\) for every \\(u&lt;\\pi/(3\\lambda)=\\pi/b\\). Fourier inversion and contour shifting give this because the first zeros of \\(D(z^2)\\) have imaginary part \\(\\pi/(3\\lambda)\\), and \\(h_j(z^2)\\) decays exponentially on narrower horizontal lines.</p><p>For \\(\\|A\\|_N\\le1\\), the area mean-value bound and comparison with \\(T_{\\lfloor N/2\\rfloor}\\) give\n\\[\n|(\\Delta^\\ell T_NA)(iy)|\n\\le C_\\ell N^{-\\ell}\n \\exp\\{-cNe^{-\\pi|y|/b}\\}.\n\\]\nIntegrate against \\(f_h\\), then use inverse-form differences:\n\\[\n\\|\\Delta^\\ell A_{h,N}\\|_N\n\\le N^{-1-\\ell+o(1)}. \\tag{5.11}\n\\]</p><p>For an input \\(C_s\\), \\(0\\le s&lt;b/2\\), the representing vector is\n\\[\nJ(s)Q(v(s))^NK_N(\\cdot,v(s)).\n\\]\nAs \\(s\\uparrow b/2\\), finite projection pairings and residual transforms on compact strip subsets converge. Thus the formula extends to \\(c_3\\) using the boundary column and \\(Q(1)=1\\), without claiming bounded boundary evaluation.</p><p>Now impose the remaining oblique constraint. Put \\(u_0=v(\\lambda/2)\\). Let \\(\\chi_N\\) average values at \\(v(t)\\), \\(0\\le t\\le\\lambda\\), with probability proportional to\n\\[\nJ(t)|Q(v(t))|^N\\,dt.\n\\]\nThe transform of \\(r_Nh\\) is \\(T_N\\) applied to\n\\[\nA_{h,N}-K_N(\\cdot,u_0)\n \\frac{\\chi_N(A_{h,N})}\n      {\\chi_N(K_N(\\cdot,u_0))}. \\tag{5.12}\n\\]\nOrthogonality against \\(M_N\\) is encoded by \\(T_N\\); the remaining condition against \\(g=\\int_0^\\lambda C_t\\,dt\\) is \\(\\chi_N=0\\), since \\(Q\\) has constant sign on this interval away from its zeros.</p><p>Every fixed \\(\\ell\\)-th difference of the averaging probabilities is \\(O_\\ell(N^{-\\ell})\\) in total variation. Divide their exponential base by its maximum. Differences insert powers of the gap to one. Analyticity gives finitely many maxima of finite order, so one-dimensional Laplace estimates compare integrals at \\(N/2\\) and \\(N\\); normalization and quotient differences give the claim.</p><p>Kernel positivity and the leading limit yield\n\\[\n\\chi_N(K_N(\\cdot,u_0))\\ge cN,\\qquad\n|\\Delta^\\ell\\chi_N(K_N(\\cdot,u_0))|\n\\le C_\\ell N^{1-\\ell}.\n\\]\nFor \\(h_j\\), the subtracted vector has the same bound as (5.11), using the \\(O(\\sqrt N)\\) interior evaluation and column-norm bounds. For \\(c_3\\), the scalar multiplier has differences \\(O_\\ell(N^{-\\ell})\\). Model columns at \\(1,u_0\\), and their finite-norm corrections, have \\(T_N\\)-images on the imaginary axis bounded by \\(O(N^{-\\ell})\\). Therefore, with\n\\[\nF_{h,N}(\\omega)=\\int(r_Nh)C_\\omega\\,d\\mu,\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\Delta^\\ell F_{h,N}(iy)|\n\\le N^{a_h-\\ell+o(1)}\n\\quad(y\\in\\mathbb R), \\tag{5.13}\n\\]\nwhere \\(a_{h_0}=a_{h_1}=-1\\) and \\(a_{c_3}=0\\).</p><p>To recover values at \\(-4\\), set\n\\[\n\\rho_{h,N}(z)=(r_Nh)(z^2)\n \\frac{zD(z^2)}{\\sinh(4\\lambda z)}.\n\\]\nFor every fixed positive \\(Y\\) not an even integer,\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}|\\rho_{h,N}(z+iY)|\\,dz\n\\le C_YN^{bY/\\pi+C}, \\tag{5.14}\n\\]\nwith additive exponent \\(C\\) independent of \\(Y\\).</p><p>Here are the growth details. In the polynomial basis (5.3) with \\(c=b\\), coefficients of an element of \\(F_{0,N}\\) beyond \\(N-1\\) have form\n\\[\nq^jp(j)+d_0q_1^j,\\qquad\n\\deg p&lt;N,\\quad 0&lt;q_1=w_{\\lambda/2}&lt;q=w_\\lambda&lt;1.\n\\]\nDivide by \\(q^j\\) and take \\(N\\) consecutive differences to bound \\(d_0\\) by \\(e^{O(N)}\\) on the unit sphere. Interpolation on \\(N\\) consecutive indices bounds \\(p\\). Coefficients consequently decay exponentially beyond \\(C'N\\).</p><p>Fix \\(2\\lambda&lt;c&lt;b\\) and repeat in the \\(\\mu_c\\) basis. Truncation at degree \\(O(N)\\) gives exponentially small relative \\(\\mu_c\\)-error. The small-circle generating bound\n\\[\n|P_j^{(c)}(z^2)|\\le C_c^{j+1}e^{cz/4}\n\\]\nputs a fixed positive fraction of the norm on \\(z\\le C'N\\). The density ratio there gives\n\\[\n\\|f\\|_{\\mu_c}\\le e^{O(N)}\\|f\\|_{\\mu_b},\n\\qquad f\\in F_{0,N}.\n\\]</p><p>Pairing with \\(c_3\\) has polynomial norm on this space. On \\(z\\le N^2\\), use Cauchy–Schwarz and\n\\(c_3(z^2)^2\\,d\\mu/dz=O(1+z)\\). Above \\(N^2\\), the \\(\\mu_c\\)-comparison and \\(e^{-(b-c)z}\\) dominate \\(e^{O(N)}\\). Thus the orthogonal and extra oblique projection terms have polynomial norms. Finally the generating formula on \\(|w_t|=e^{-1/(j+1)}\\) gives\n\\[\n|P_j((z+iY)^2)|\n\\le C_Y(j+1)^{1/2+bY/\\pi}e^{b|z|/2}.\n\\]\nThe parameter obeys \\(|\\operatorname{Re}t|&lt;b/2\\) and\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{Im}t|\\le(b/\\pi)\\log(j+1)+O(1).\n\\]\nSum using coefficient bounds and multiply by the density to obtain (5.14), with integrable exponential decay.</p><p>Fix \\(\\ell\\), choose arbitrarily small \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), and put\n\\[\nS_N=(b/\\pi+\\eta)\\log N.\n\\]\nShift the full even Fourier contour to \\(\\operatorname{Im}z=Y\\). The crossed poles are the simple poles of \\(1/\\sinh(4\\lambda z)\\) at \\(z=2in\\), \\(n\\ge1\\). For \\(y\\ge S_N\\),\n\\[\nF_{h,N}(iy)=\n \\sum_{0&lt;2n&lt;Y}b_{n,N}e^{-2ny}\n +O_Y(e^{-Yy}N^{bY/\\pi+C}), \\tag{5.15}\n\\]\nwhere \\(b_{1,N}\\) is a fixed nonzero multiple of \\(r_Nh(-4)\\).</p><p>Apply the same expansion at \\(N,\\ldots,N+\\ell\\), using the same \\(S_N\\), and difference. Evaluate at \\(S_N+j\\) for enough consecutive integers \\(j\\); the exponentials give a fixed Vandermonde system. Choose non-even \\(Y\\) large depending on \\(\\ell,\\eta\\). Since \\(C\\) is independent of \\(Y\\), the remainder \\(N^{-\\eta Y+C}\\) is negligible. Equation (5.13) gives\n\\[\n|\\Delta^\\ell r_Nh(-4)|\n\\le N^{a_h+2b/\\pi+2\\eta-\\ell+o(1)}.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\eta\\) be arbitrarily small.</p><p>The same expansion bounds the integral over \\(y\\ge S_N\\) by \\(N^{a_h-\\ell+o(1)}\\). Below \\(S_N\\), integrate (5.13), absorbing its logarithm into \\(o(1)\\). Fourier inversion at \\(z=0\\), where the density is positive and finite, gives the stated estimates at zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Uniform nonzero denominator; last line of original equation (5.10).", "statement": "<p>Uniform nonzero denominator; last line of original equation (5.10).</p><p>Under Lemma 8.1's setting,\n\\[\n-r_Nc_3(0)\\ge c&gt;0.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(r_N(c_3-h_1)(0)\\) is bounded away from zero for all sufficiently large \\(N\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>First replace \\(G_N\\) by \\(G_{N,t}\\), \\(0&lt;t&lt;\\lambda\\), and use \\(W\\) for projection and transform. Apart from fixed positive factor \\(J(b/2)\\), the residual transform is \\(J(iy)Q(v(iy))^N\\) times\n\\[\nK_N^0(v,1)-K_N^0(v,u_0)\n \\frac{K_N^0(v(t),1)}{K_N^0(v(t),u_0)},\n\\qquad v=v(iy).\n\\]\nAfter division by \\(N\\), Lemma 8.3 gives a limit uniform on the imaginary diameter. At \\(v=\\pm i\\), its sign is the sign of\n\\[\n\\frac12-\n\\frac{1-v(t)^2u_0^2}\n     {(1+u_0^2)(1-v(t)^2)}.\n\\]\nThis is uniformly strictly negative for \\(0\\le t\\le\\lambda\\), since the second term is at least \\(1/(1+u_0^2)&gt;1/2\\). The remaining \\(O\\)-factors are positive and uniformly bounded above and below.</p><p>Fourier inversion at zero integrates the bracket divided by \\(N\\) against\n\\[\ncN J(iy)Q(v(iy))^N\\,dy.\n\\]\nThis positive measure has mass bounded above and below and concentrates at \\(y\\to\\pm\\infty\\), because on the imaginary diameter\n\\[\nQ\\ge0,\\qquad 1-Q\\asymp e^{-\\pi|y|/b},\n\\qquad J\\asymp e^{-\\pi|y|/b}.\n\\]\nThe uniform kernel bound and negative endpoint limit give the lower bound for \\(W\\), uniformly in \\(t\\).</p><p>The exact ratio is\n\\[\n\\frac{d\\mu}{dW}\n=\\frac{(2u-1)^2(2u+1)}{4u(2u^2-1)},\n\\qquad u=\\cosh(\\lambda\\sqrt x).\n\\]\nIt is positive, smooth, and eventually increasing. With \\(r(x)=(x+2)/(x+1)\\), choose fixed integer \\(j&gt;0\\) so \\(\\mu/(r^jW)\\) is increasing: on a compact interval the negative logarithmic derivative is bounded and \\(-(\\log r)'&gt;0\\); outside it the original derivative is nonnegative.</p><p>Ordered association bounds the expectation of \\(-e_0c_3\\) for \\(\\mu\\) below by that for \\(r^jW\\). Each multiplication by \\(r\\) changes this expectation by a bounded factor: its ratio is the ratio of characteristic-product expectations under \\((A_N,G_{N,t})\\) and \\((F_{0,N},G_{N,t})\\). Lemma 8.1's interlacing comparisons reduce them to \\((U_N,U_N)\\) and \\((M_N,M_N)\\), and symmetric codimension-one compression compares these. After fixed \\(j\\) steps, a positive lower bound remains. Integrate the positive \\(G_{N,t}\\)-mixture to obtain the bound for \\(G_N\\).</p><p>Finally Lemma 8.5 gives \\(|r_Nh_1(0)|\\le N^{-1+o(1)}\\), proving the denominator assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 6.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(\\eta>0\\), there is \\(C_\\eta<\\infty\\) such that \\( p(H=n)\\le C_\\eta n^{-7/4+\\eta} \\qquad(n\\ge1). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Under the irreducible probability \\(p\\), let \\(H\\) be one piece's positive integral height.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the renewal identity\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{m\\ge0}B_mz^m=\\frac1{1-\\mathbb E_pz^H},\n  \\]\n  the height tail of Proposition 2, and strip regularity of Proposition 8.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), there is \\(C_\\eta&lt;\\infty\\) such that\n\\[\np(H=n)\\le C_\\eta n^{-7/4+\\eta}\n\\qquad(n\\ge1).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(b(z)=\\sum_{m\\ge0}B_mz^m\\). Fix \\(n\\ge2\\), set \\(r=e^{-1/n}\\), and let\n\\[\ns=\\min\\{1,n^{-1}+|\\theta|\\},\n\\qquad -\\pi\\le\\theta\\le\\pi.\n\\]\nThe tail gives\n\\[\n|1-\\mathbb E_p(re^{i\\theta})^H|\n\\le C\\mathbb E_p\\min\\{1,sH\\}\\le Cs^{3/4},\n\\]\nso \\(|b(re^{i\\theta})|\\ge cs^{-3/4}\\); there is no zero on the circle.</p><p>For each fixed \\(k\\ge0\\) and \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), Proposition 8 implies\n\\[\n|\\partial_\\theta^kb(re^{i\\theta})|\n\\le C_{k,\\delta}s^{-3/4-k-\\delta}. \\tag{6.3}\n\\]\nIndeed use a smooth dyadic partition on positive integers. On \\(m\\asymp M\\), the \\(j\\)-th difference of \\(m^kB_mr^m\\) times the cutoff is at most\n\\[\nC_{j,k,\\delta}M^{k-1/4-j+\\delta}e^{-M/(Cn)}.\n\\]\nSummation by parts \\(j\\) times, together with the trivial sum bound, gives\n\\[\nC_{j,k,\\delta}M^{3/4+k+\\delta}\n e^{-M/(Cn)}(1+M|\\theta|)^{-j}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(j&gt;3/4+k+\\delta\\) and sum dyadically, separating \\(M\\le s^{-1}\\).</p><p>Differentiate the reciprocal twice:\n\\[\n|\\partial_\\theta^2b(re^{i\\theta})^{-1}|\n\\le C_\\delta s^{-5/4-2\\delta}.\n\\]\nIts integral is at most \\(C_\\delta n^{1/4+2\\delta}\\). Since\n\\(\\mathbb E_pz^H=1-b(z)^{-1}\\), integrate the \\(n\\)-th Fourier coefficient by parts twice:\n\\[\nr^np(H=n)\n\\le\\frac1{2\\pi n^2}\n \\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}\n |\\partial_\\theta^2b(re^{i\\theta})^{-1}|\\,d\\theta\n\\le C_\\delta n^{-7/4+2\\delta}.\n\\]\nPeriodicity kills boundary terms, \\(r^n=e^{-1}\\), and \\(\\delta\\) is arbitrary.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition 6.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is \\(C<\\infty\\) such that, for every \\(k\\ge1\\), \\( \\sup_w\\Pr(S_k=w)\\le Ck^{-8/3}, \\qquad \\sum_{m\\in\\mathbb Z}\\sup_{H(w)=m}\\Pr(S_k=w) \\le Ck^{-4/3}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Under the irreducible probability \\(p\\), write \\(X=(H,Z)\\) for height and transverse displacement on its actual row-staggered lattice.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S_k=X_1+\\cdots+X_k\\) for independent whole pieces.</p><p class=\"li\">For a displacement \\(w\\), write \\(H(w)\\) for its height coordinate.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(C&lt;\\infty\\) such that, for every \\(k\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_w\\Pr(S_k=w)\\le Ck^{-8/3},\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{m\\in\\mathbb Z}\\sup_{H(w)=m}\\Pr(S_k=w)\n\\le Ck^{-4/3}. \\tag{6.4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First establish fixed \\(0&lt;c_0&lt;C_0&lt;\\infty\\), \\(c_1&gt;0\\), uniformly over unit vectors \\(e\\) and large \\(x\\), such that\n\\[\np\\{c_0x\\le|e\\cdot X|\\le C_0x\\}\\ge c_1x^{-3/4}. \\tag{6.5}\n\\]\nUse bridges with heights in \\([x,2x]\\) and terminal transverse intervals of length comparable to \\(x\\). Proposition 3 gives mass \\(cx^{-5/4}\\) at each port. Positive- and negative-slope intervals can be chosen so displacements have projection at least a fixed multiple of \\(x\\) in any prescribed direction. Compactness of the unit circle makes constants uniform. Summing heights and ports gives mass \\(cx^{3/4}\\).</p><p>By renewal factorization this is a sum of probabilities for \\(S_k\\). Terms with \\(k&gt;Ax^{3/4}\\) total at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{k&gt;Ax^{3/4}}e^2(\\mathbb E_pe^{-H/x})^k\n\\le Cx^{3/4}e^{-cA}.\n\\]\nFix large \\(A\\). Some \\(k\\le Ax^{3/4}\\) has a fixed positive probability of total absolute projection comparable to \\(x\\). The probability of an increment with \\(D&gt;C_0x\\) is at most \\(CAC_0^{-3/4}\\), while\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{i\\le k}|e\\cdot X_i|\n \\mathbf1_{\\{|e\\cdot X_i|&lt;c_0x\\}}\n\\le CAc_0^{1/4}x.\n\\]\nChoose \\(C_0\\) large and \\(c_0\\) small. A window increment occurs with fixed positive probability. A union bound and \\(k\\le Ax^{3/4}\\) give (6.5).</p><p>In fixed integer coordinates for the displacement lattice, let\n\\(\\varphi(\\theta)=\\mathbb E e^{i\\theta\\cdot X}\\). An independent \\(X'\\) is bounded with positive probability. Apply (6.5) in direction \\(\\theta/|\\theta|\\), with \\(x\\) a small fixed multiple of \\(|\\theta|^{-1}\\). Then\n\\[\n1-|\\varphi(\\theta)|^2\n=\\mathbb E[1-\\cos(\\theta\\cdot(X-X'))]\n\\ge c|\\theta|^{3/4}\n\\]\nnear zero. Directional windows make the affine support two-dimensional, so its difference group is a full-rank lattice subgroup. There are finitely many modulus-one torus points, with translated copies of the estimate; elsewhere the modulus is strictly below one. Fourier inversion yields\n\\[\n\\sup_w\\Pr(S_k=w)\n\\le C\\int_0^1e^{-ckr^{3/4}}r\\,dr+Ce^{-ck}\n\\le Ck^{-8/3}.\n\\]</p><p>For the second bound, define for a nonnegative measure \\(q\\)\n\\[\n\\|q\\|_{1,\\infty}\n=\\sum_m\\sup_{H(w)=m}q(w).\n\\]\nThen \\(\\|q*r\\|_{1,\\infty}\\le\\|q\\|_{1,\\infty}\\|r\\|_1\\). The planar atom bound summed over \\(m\\le2k^{4/3}\\) gives \\(Ck^{-4/3}\\).</p><p>For a dyadic shell \\(m\\asymp\\Lambda k^{4/3}\\), \\(\\Lambda\\ge2\\), divide the \\(k\\) pieces into sixteen independent comparable blocks; bounded \\(k\\) is absorbed into constants. Each block has atoms at most \\(Ck^{-8/3}\\), and its height exceeds \\(t\\ge k^{4/3}\\) with probability at most \\(Ckt^{-3/4}\\), by tail splitting and truncated first moment.</p><p>If one block height is at most \\(k^{4/3}\\sqrt\\Lambda\\), another is at least \\(c\\Lambda k^{4/3}\\) for large \\(\\Lambda\\). The restricted first block has mixed norm at most \\(C\\sqrt\\Lambda\\,k^{-4/3}\\); the latter event costs \\(C\\Lambda^{-3/4}\\). Convolution and finitely many choices give \\(C\\Lambda^{-1/4}k^{-4/3}\\).</p><p>If all sixteen block heights exceed \\(k^{4/3}\\sqrt\\Lambda\\), impose the atom bound on one and probability \\(C\\Lambda^{-3/8}\\) on each of the other fifteen. There are \\(O(\\Lambda k^{4/3})\\) heights, giving\n\\[\nC\\Lambda^{-37/8}k^{-4/3}.\n\\]\nBoth shell bounds are dyadically summable. Enlarge constants for small shells and bounded \\(k\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Theorem 7.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(\\epsilon>0\\), \\( \\Pr\\{h^{4/3-\\epsilon}\\le L\\le h^{4/3+\\epsilon}\\} \\longrightarrow1 \\) uniformly under the fixed displacement bound.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a bound on transverse displacement divided by height.</p><p class=\"li\">Take compatible prescribed ports on two parallel pure cuts at integral height \\(h\\to\\infty\\), with displacement obeying this bound.</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize the critical bridge weights by their prescribed-endpoint mass.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr\\{h^{4/3-\\epsilon}\\le L\\le h^{4/3+\\epsilon}\\}\n\\longrightarrow1\n\\]\nuniformly under the fixed displacement bound.</p><p>The conclusion also holds in convex pure-sided domains in a compact family of nondegenerate scaled geometries, with ports on opposite parallel sides and order-\\(h\\) clearance from every other side.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(w\\) be the prescribed displacement, \\(H(w)=h\\), and put \\(T_k=\\sum_{i\\le k}L_i\\). Unique factorization gives bridge mass\n\\[\n\\sum_{k\\ge1}\\Pr(S_k=w).\n\\]\nProposition 3 gives its normalizer at least \\(ch^{-5/4}\\), also in the finite domains. It remains to bound absolute strip exceptional mass by \\(o(h^{-5/4})\\).</p><p>Fix small \\(\\xi&gt;0\\), put \\(K=h^{3/4-\\xi}\\), and choose\n\\[\na=h^{1-\\tau},\\qquad0&lt;\\tau&lt;8\\xi/21.\n\\]\nFor \\(k\\le K\\), the height-\\(h\\) event with every \\(H_i&lt;a\\) costs at most\n\\[\ne^{-h/a}(\\mathbb E[e^{H/a};H&lt;a])^k\n\\le\\exp\\{-h^\\tau+Cka^{-3/4}\\}.\n\\]\nUse \\(e^t-1\\le et\\) on \\([0,1]\\) and\n\\(\\mathbb E[H;H&lt;a]\\le Ca^{1/4}\\). Since\n\\(ka^{-3/4}\\le h^{-\\xi+3\\tau/4}\\), summation is smaller than every inverse power.</p><p>If \\(H_i\\ge a\\), condition on other pieces. Propositions 9–10 give\n\\[\n\\Pr(S_k=w,H_i\\ge a)\n\\le\\sup_{n\\ge a}p(H=n)\n \\sum_m\\sup_{H(v)=m}\\Pr(S_{k-1}=v)\n\\le a^{-7/4+o(1)}C(1+k)^{-4/3}.\n\\]\nFor \\(k=1\\), interpret the remainder as unit mass at zero. Sum candidate indices and \\(k\\le K\\):\n\\[\nCa^{-7/4+o(1)}K^{2/3}\n=h^{-5/4-2\\xi/3+7\\tau/4+o(1)}\n=o(h^{-5/4}).\n\\]</p><p>For \\(k&gt;h^{3/4+\\xi}\\), height damping gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{k&gt;h^{3/4+\\xi}}\n \\Pr\\Big\\{\\sum_{i\\le k}H_i=h\\Big\\}\n\\le e\\sum_{k&gt;h^{3/4+\\xi}}(\\mathbb Ee^{-H/h})^k\n\\le Ch^{3/4}e^{-ch^\\xi}.\n\\]</p><p>Remain with \\(h^{3/4-\\xi}&lt;k\\le h^{3/4+\\xi}\\). For \\(\\xi\\) small relative to \\(\\epsilon\\), the length deficit at \\(u=h^{-4/3+\\epsilon}\\) gives\n\\[\n\\Pr(T_k&lt;h^{4/3-\\epsilon})\n\\le e(\\mathbb Ee^{-uL})^k\n\\le\\exp\\{-h^{9\\epsilon/16-\\xi-o(1)}\\}.\n\\]\nIts sum is negligible.</p><p>For the upper tail, split into two independent comparable batches. If \\(T_k&gt;h^{4/3+\\epsilon}\\), one batch exceeds \\(t=\\frac12h^{4/3+\\epsilon}\\). Tail and truncation give, for fixed small \\(\\eta&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr(\\text{batch length}&gt;t)\n\\le C_\\eta kt^{-9/16+\\eta}\\le h^{-\\delta}\n\\]\nfor some \\(\\delta&gt;0\\). On the other batch impose the displacement atom at cost \\(Ck^{-8/3}\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\sum_{k&gt;h^{3/4-\\xi}}\n C h^{-\\delta}k^{-8/3}\n\\le Ch^{-5/4+5\\xi/3-\\delta}\n=o(h^{-5/4})\n\\]\nafter decreasing \\(\\xi\\). This separates the length and endpoint costs without asserting coordinate independence or post-conditioning independence.</p><p>Divide by \\(ch^{-5/4}\\). Restriction to a finite domain decreases exceptional mass and retains the same connector lower bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Theorem 8.1 (Tilted prescribed endpoints)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Under the prescribed critical chord law, \\( L(\\gamma)=R^{4/3+o(1)} \\quad\\text{in probability}. \\) The conclusion persists after restriction to the hexagons of Theorem 1 with the same side-interior limiting data.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W\\) be a pure wedge of angle \\(\\pi/3\\) or \\(2\\pi/3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Take prescribed ports \\(a,Q\\), one on each ray, with order-\\(R\\) clearance from the opposite support and distance \\(O(R)\\) from the apex.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n,m\\) be the inward unit normals at source and target, respectively.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(K_U=K(a,U;W)\\) for critical mass at a specified target-ray port \\(U\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Constants are uniform over a fixed compact set of nondegenerate scaled geometries.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Under the prescribed critical chord law,\n\\[\nL(\\gamma)=R^{4/3+o(1)}\n\\quad\\text{in probability}.\n\\]\nThe conclusion persists after restriction to the hexagons of Theorem 1 with the same side-interior limiting data.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma 12.4, up to mass \\(o(R^{-5/4})\\), use a small prefix with\n\\(k=\\lfloor R^{3/4-\\eta}\\rfloor\\) pieces followed by a chord in \\(W'\\).</p><p>Under the independent law, prefix length has exponent\n\\[\n\\frac{3/4-\\eta}{9/16}\n=\\frac43-\\frac{16\\eta}{9}\n\\]\nin probability. On small prefixes, Lemma 12.3 bounds the appended kernel by \\(CR^{-5/4}\\), uniformly in displacement. An independent event of probability \\(o(1)\\) therefore gives constructed mass \\(o(R^{-5/4})\\), without independence after fixing \\(Q\\). Choose \\(\\eta&lt;9\\epsilon/16\\). This proves the short tail and makes prefixes longer than \\(\\frac12R^{4/3+\\epsilon}\\) negligible.</p><p>For the appended long chord, Proposition 10 bounds each specific prefix displacement by \\(O(k^{-8/3})\\). Align new wedges by lattice translations \\(\\tau_w\\), their apex intersections being triangular lattice vertices. The aligned endpoints are\n\\[\nx_w=a+w+\\tau_w,\\qquad y_w=Q+\\tau_w,\n\\qquad w=(x_w-y_w)-(a-Q).\n\\]\nThus an endpoint pair determines \\(w\\). Since \\(|w|=O(R)\\) and angles are fixed, \\(\\tau_w=O(R)\\), and there are only \\(O(R)\\) possible aligned source ports.</p><p>Sum freely over endpoint pairs. With \\(S_*=R^{1+\\lambda}\\), appended chords of diameter above \\(S_*\\) cost at most \\(CRS_*^{-1/4}\\), by one-source diameter bounds. Smaller chords lie in a common convex pure truncation of diameter \\(O(S_*)\\); those longer than \\(\\frac12R^{4/3+\\epsilon}\\) cost at most\n\\[\nCR^{-4/3-\\epsilon}S_*^{25/12+o(1)}.\n\\]\nMultiply by \\(k^{-8/3}\\). The two bounds become\n\\[\nCR^{-5/4-\\lambda/4+8\\eta/3},\n\\qquad\nR^{-5/4-\\epsilon+25\\lambda/12+8\\eta/3+o(1)}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\lambda&gt;0\\) with \\(25\\lambda/12&lt;\\epsilon\\), then \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) sufficiently small that both exponents are below \\(-5/4\\), retaining \\(\\eta&lt;9\\epsilon/16\\).</p><p>Both exceptions are \\(o(R^{-5/4})\\). Proposition 3 supplies the normalizer. Restriction to a hexagon decreases absolute exceptions and retains its own \\(cR^{-5/4}\\) connector mass.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The function \\(F\\) is decreasing, \\( F(r)\\lesssim(1+r)^{-1/4},\\qquad F(r+o(r))-F(r)=o(r^{-1/4}), \\) and \\( \\sum_UE_U=o(R^{-1/4}). \\)", "statement": "<p>Terminal-summed prefix exhaustion; original location: opening of Section 8.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In Proposition 12's wedge, let \\(r\\) be the distance from \\(a\\) to the target support in row-spacing units.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(F(r)=\\sum_UK_U\\), summed along the target ray.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(k\\) independent \\(n\\)-directed irreducibles, call the prefix small when the sum of their enlarged diameters is less than a sufficiently small fixed multiple of both endpoint clearances.</p><p class=\"li\">For its displacement \\(w\\), put \\(a'=a+w\\), and let \\(W'\\subset W\\) be the wedge whose initial support passes through \\(a'\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(k=\\lfloor R^{3/4-\\eta}\\rfloor\\), \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) fixed and small, let \\(E_U\\) be the mass at \\(U\\) of paths not obtained by a small \\(k\\)-piece prefix followed by a chord in \\(W'\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The function \\(F\\) is decreasing,\n\\[\nF(r)\\lesssim(1+r)^{-1/4},\\qquad\nF(r+o(r))-F(r)=o(r^{-1/4}),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_UE_U=o(R^{-1/4}).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>With directions fixed, \\(r\\), on its positive half-odd support levels, determines \\(F\\) by translation. Keeping the source support fixed, increasing \\(r\\) expands the wedge. The real contour identity is\n\\[\n1=\\cos(\\sigma\\pi)A(r)+\\cos(\\sigma\\theta)F(r),\n\\qquad \\sigma=3/8,\n\\]\nwhere \\(A\\) sums exits to the initial support and \\(\\theta\\) is the opening angle. Turns to the other side have magnitude \\(\\theta\\), and distant truncating exits vanish by the diameter bound. Monotonicity of \\(A\\) gives decreasing \\(F\\); Proposition 1 gives its upper bound.</p><p>For any deterministic \\(k\\), append chords in \\(W'\\) to small prefixes:\n\\[\nF(r)\\ge\n\\mathbb E[\\mathbf1_{\\mathrm{small}}F(r+m\\cdot w/d)].\n\\]\nThe prefix avoids the target line and is recovered by its first \\(k\\) positive singly crossed source-parallel levels; the last cut strictly separates the appended chord.</p><p>For each large \\(q\\), some \\(k\\le Cq^{3/4}\\) has probability bounded below of\n\\[\nm\\cdot w/d\\le-b_0q.\n\\]\nIndeed Proposition 4 with second direction \\(-m\\) gives such bridges over heights \\([q,2q]\\) with total mass \\(\\gtrsim q^{3/4}\\); equivalently use point-to-point corridors to suitable top intervals. Counts above \\(Cq^{3/4}\\) cost \\(O(q^{3/4}e^{-cC})\\), by height damping. At these counts, diameter sum exceeds \\(Tq\\) with probability \\(O(T^{-3/4})\\).</p><p>If \\(F\\) dropped by \\(\\epsilon r^{-1/4}\\) across \\(r\\pm l\\), \\(1\\le l=o(r)\\), choose fixed large \\(A_0\\), then \\(l\\ll q\\ll r\\) with variation\n\\[\no(r^{-1/4})\n\\quad\\text{on}\\quad\n[r+b_0q/4,\\ r+(A_0+b_0+1)q].\n\\]\nMonotonicity and arbitrarily many disjoint geometric bands permit this. Apply the construction inequality at \\(j\\) near \\(r+b_0q/2\\). Small prefixes with \\(|m\\cdot w/d|\\le A_0q\\) lose only \\(o(r^{-1/4})\\); those shifting at most \\(-b_0q\\) gain \\(\\epsilon r^{-1/4}\\). Smallness tends to probability one, and the remaining offset loss is arbitrarily small for large \\(A_0\\). Since \\(F(j)\\lesssim r^{-1/4}\\), this is impossible.</p><p>For \\(k=\\lfloor R^{3/4-\\eta}\\rfloor\\), truncation makes total diameter \\(o(R)\\) in probability. Continuity and \\(r\\asymp R\\) show that constructed mass differs from \\(F(r)\\) by \\(o(R^{-1/4})\\), proving the exceptional-mass sum.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( H_q(t)\\lesssim R^{-1/4}(t/q)^{3/8}. \\)", "statement": "<p>Local visit estimate; original equation (8.1).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 12's compact wedge geometries.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(q\\le cR\\), with \\(c&gt;0\\) sufficiently small.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(1\\le t\\le q\\), let \\(H_q(t)\\) be the mass of paths from \\(a\\) to target-ray ports \\(P\\) at distance between \\(q\\) and \\(2q\\) from \\(Q\\), which also come within distance \\(t\\) of \\(Q\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nH_q(t)\\lesssim R^{-1/4}(t/q)^{3/8}. \\tag{8.1}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Proposition 6's fixed-source construction at a small fixed multiple of scale \\(q\\). Arrange every bridge excursion and translation below \\(q/20\\), while both support gaps remain order \\(q\\). Bounded \\(q\\) follows directly from the diameter bound. The first summed indicator mass is at least\n\\[\nc_1q^{1/2}H_q(t),\n\\]\nusing \\(q^{3/4}\\) side-1 summed mass and \\(q^{-1/4}\\) side-2 mass. The output measure has total mass \\(O(R^{-1/4})\\).</p><p>For two translations \\(v,v'\\), recover both support crossings as in Proposition 6. For \\(t\\le q/10\\), visits near either \\(Q+v\\) or \\(Q+v'\\) cannot lie on exterior branches: initial branches remain order \\(R\\) away, and terminal branches stay within \\(q/20\\) of translated endpoints in the distance-\\(q\\) bin. Both visits therefore occur in the common central portion.</p><p>That portion stays above both target cuts. Visiting within \\(t\\) of the landmark on the lower cut forces\n\\[\n|m\\cdot(v-v')|\\le Ct.\n\\]\nThe mixed-cut central chord retains the visit to the landmark of its own terminal cut; its endpoint remains in the same distance bin and its start is prescribed. Its geometry changes only by \\(O(q)\\).</p><p>Suppose the visit estimate is known with exponent \\(\\alpha\\), initially \\(\\alpha=0\\) by diameter. Then central mass is at most\n\\[\nCR^{-1/4}(t/q)^\\alpha.\n\\]\nThe restricted difference sum is\n\\[\n\\sum_{z:\\,|m\\cdot z|\\le Ct}\n D_n(z)(1+|m\\cdot z|)^{-1/4}\n\\le C't^{1/2}.\n\\]\nIndeed Proposition 5 bounds the \\(j\\)-piece contribution by\n\\[\nC\\min\\{j^{-1/3},(1+t)^{3/4}j^{-4/3}\\},\n\\]\nwhose renewal sum, including ties, is \\(O(t^{1/2})\\).</p><p>The outer factors are \\(O(q^{3/4})\\) and \\(O(q^{-1/4})\\). Dropping mutual avoidance gives second moment at most\n\\[\nCq^{1/2}t^{1/2}R^{-1/4}(t/q)^\\alpha.\n\\]\nCauchy–Schwarz upgrades the exponent to \\(1/4+\\alpha/2\\). The range \\(t\\ge q/10\\) follows by diameter. Two iterations give \\(3/8\\). Each iteration shifts supports only by \\(O(q)\\), so slightly enlarge compact bounds and reduce \\(c\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "With integrals denoting critical weighted sums, \\( \\left| \\int_{a\\to Q}f_W(P;\\omega) -\\int_{a\\to P}f_W(Q;\\omega) \\right| \\le CR^{-1/4}(K_Q+K_P). \\) For paths to \\(Q\\) …", "statement": "<p>Endpointwise artificial-arc comparison; original location: Section 8.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Take target-ray ports \\(P,Q\\), with \\(P\\) nearer the apex and \\(|P-Q|\\in[q,2q]\\), \\(q\\le cR\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Add a formal exterior semicircle from \\(P\\) to \\(Q\\), tangent to normal rays at its anchors. It is not a honeycomb path; give it modulus one, no length, and phase \\(e^{i\\sigma\\Delta W}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Orient coordinates so the initial ray points right and the target ray is counterclockwise by angle \\(\\theta\\). Then \\(a\\to P,a\\to Q\\) turn by \\(\\theta\\), and the arc \\(P\\to Q\\) turns by \\(-\\pi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\omega:a\\to Q\\), define\n  \\[\n  f_W(P;\\omega)=\\frac1{2\\cos(\\sigma\\pi)}\n  -\\sum_{b'\\in(P,Q)}\n    \\sum_{\\substack{\\zeta:P\\to b'\\text{ in }W\\\\\n                    \\zeta\\text{ disjoint from }\\omega}}\n      \\kappa^{L(\\zeta)}.\n  \\]\n  Define \\(f_W(Q;\\omega)\\) analogously for \\(\\omega:a\\to P\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>With integrals denoting critical weighted sums,\n\\[\n\\left|\n \\int_{a\\to Q}f_W(P;\\omega)\n -\\int_{a\\to P}f_W(Q;\\omega)\n\\right|\n\\le CR^{-1/4}(K_Q+K_P).\n\\]\nFor paths to \\(Q\\),\n\\[\nc'q^{-1/4}\\le f_W(P;\\omega)\n\\le C(1+\\min\\{q,\\operatorname{dist}(P,\\omega)\\})^{-1/4}, \\tag{8.2}\n\\]\nand likewise with \\(P,Q\\) exchanged. Consequently\n\\[\nK_Q\\lesssim R^{-5/4}\n\\]\nuniformly in the stated compact geometries.</p>", "proof": "<p>Sum only paths using the exterior arc exactly once. Ordinary prefix cancellations preserve the count of arc uses under unfinished-loop reversal. The arc lies well after the initial cut, so the source cannot be inside a loop. Work first in a large convex truncation; distant contributions vanish by diameter bounds.</p><p>Flux delivered by arc traversal equals outgoing flux with one arc use. After rotation by \\(e^{-i\\sigma\\theta}\\), the imaginary input is\n\\[\n\\sin(\\sigma\\pi)(K_Q-K_P).\n\\]\nOutputs to the initial support cost \\(CR^{-1/4}(K_Q+K_P)\\). For an initial \\(a\\to Q\\) followed by the arc to \\(P\\), noncrossing forces target exits nearer than \\(Q\\). Exits before \\(P\\) have relative phase \\(1\\); exits in \\((P,Q)\\) have imaginary part \\(\\sin(2\\sigma\\pi)\\), from a final positive-\\(\\pi\\) arch turn. Initial \\(a\\to P\\) contributes the opposite sign on \\((P,Q)\\). Anchors cannot be final exits because their triangles are used. Divide by \\(\\sin(2\\sigma\\pi)\\) to obtain the comparison.</p><p>Reflection and the half-plane contour identity give total one-direction arch mass \\(1/(2\\cos(\\sigma\\pi))\\). The completion sum omits at least the long-offset tail of mass \\(\\gtrsim q^{-1/4}\\), giving the lower bound in (8.2). All sufficiently small-diameter completions up to a fixed multiple of\n\\(\\min\\{q,\\operatorname{dist}(P,\\omega)\\}\\) are included by clearance and disjointness. The diameter tail bounds the loss and gives the upper bound.</p><p>Take \\(q\\) a sufficiently small fixed fraction of \\(R\\), so the comparison absorbs its \\(O(R^{-1/4})K_Q\\) term. Average over order \\(q\\) choices of \\(P\\). Lemma 12.2's exponent \\(3/8&gt;1/4\\) pays the upper singular weight by dyadic summation, giving total upper integral\n\\[\nO(R^{-1/4}q^{-1/4}),\n\\]\nincluding bounded distances and paths remaining beyond \\(q\\). Divide by the order-\\(q\\) averaging and lower weight \\(cq^{-1/4}\\) to obtain \\(K_Q\\lesssim R^{-5/4}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Removal of exceptional mass; original location:", "statement": "<p>Removal of exceptional mass; original location: Section 8.3.</p><p>For the exceptional masses \\(E_U\\) of Lemma 12.1,\n\\[\nE_Q=o(R^{-5/4})\n\\]\nat each prescribed target \\(Q\\), uniformly over the compact nondegenerate geometries.</p>", "proof": "<p>The absolute comparison of Lemma 12.3 remains valid with both left integrals restricted to exceptional paths and right error still\n\\[\nCR^{-1/4}(K_Q+K_P),\n\\]\nwith \\(C\\) independent of \\(q\\le cR\\).</p><p>To see this, subtract the signed comparison in \\(W'\\), starting at \\(a'\\), integrated over small prefixes. Prefix injection bounds its right error by the same amount. The difference between completion factors for a central path and the full constructed path costs only \\(O(R^{-1/4})\\) per path. Central completions stay beyond the prefix cut and avoid the prefix; a discarded full-domain completion must reach that cut from clearance order \\(R\\). Smallness makes constants uniform.</p><p>Fix \\(q\\asymp\\rho R\\), with small fixed \\(\\rho&gt;0\\), and put\n\\[\ne_R=R^{1/4}\\sum_UE_U\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nLet \\(I_R(q)\\) sum, over order-\\(q\\) choices of \\(P\\), the exceptional integral of \\(f_W(Q;\\omega)\\). For \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1/2\\), paths staying at least \\(\\delta q\\) from \\(Q\\) contribute\n\\[\nC(\\delta q)^{-1/4}\\sum_PE_P.\n\\]\nFor nearer paths, discard exceptional restriction and use Lemma 12.2 in dyadic distance bins. Since \\(3/8-1/4=1/8&gt;0\\), including the bounded-distance bin gives\n\\[\nI_R(q)\\le\nCR^{-1/4}q^{-1/4}\n (\\delta^{-1/4}e_R+\\delta^{1/8}).\n\\]\nFirst send \\(R\\to\\infty\\) at fixed \\(\\rho,\\delta\\), then \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\). Thus\n\\[\nI_R(q)=o(R^{-1/4}q^{-1/4}).\n\\]</p><p>Average the exceptional comparison, using (8.2) and the individual kernel upper bound:\n\\[\nq^{-1/4}E_Q\n\\le o(R^{-1/4}q^{-5/4})+CR^{-1/4}R^{-5/4}.\n\\]\nThe little-oh may depend on \\(\\rho\\), but \\(C\\) does not. Hence\n\\[\n\\limsup_{R\\to\\infty}R^{5/4}E_Q\\le C\\rho^{1/4}.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\rho\\downarrow0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Proposition 9.2 (Calibrated renewal inputs), together with the finite calibrated inputs stated before it", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The finite package gives \\( B_h\\asymp h^{-1/4},\\qquad K_g\\asymp g^{-5/4}, \\) with the arch lower bound retained at diameter at most \\(Ag\\) for fixed \\(A\\), an exponential lateral cutoff in a fixed-height strip, and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the port-rooted irreducible probability.</p><p class=\"li\">In this proposition and Propositions 14–16, write \\(Y,Z,L,D\\) for one piece's integral height, lateral displacement, length, and diameter.</p><p class=\"li\">The calibrated finite estimates are [12, Theorem 1.2 and Proposition 11.1]; their stated renewal consequences are [15, Proposition 4.1, Lemma 4.2, Lemmas 7.2 and 7.4, Section 7.3, and Theorem 7.1].</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The finite package gives\n\\[\nB_h\\asymp h^{-1/4},\\qquad K_g\\asymp g^{-5/4},\n\\]\nwith the arch lower bound retained at diameter at most \\(Ag\\) for fixed \\(A\\), an exponential lateral cutoff in a fixed-height strip, and total chord length mass at most \\(CR^{25/12}\\) in a convex box of size \\(R\\).</p><p>Under the irreducible probability, \\(2Z\\) is integral and\n\\[\n\\Pr(D&gt;r)\\le Cr^{-3/4},\\qquad\n1-\\mathbb Ee^{-tY}\\asymp t^{3/4},\\qquad\n1-\\mathbb Ee^{-tL}=t^{9/16+o(1)}.\n\\]</p><p>(i) Independent whole pieces satisfy\n\\[\n\\sup_j\\Pr(S_Y(k)=j)\\le Ck^{-4/3},\n\\qquad\n\\sup_w\\Pr(S_{(Y,Z)}(k)=w)\\le Ck^{-8/3}.\n\\]\nOff-axis bridge constructions give positive directional-window mass.</p><p>(ii) Start two independent sequences at the same bottom port through the same first center \\(v\\). Retain completed pieces below \\(s\\), excluding the first piece reaching or passing \\(s\\). Fix left and right roles. At every pair of renewal levels \\(0&lt;d_1&lt;d_2&lt;s\\), from the left and right histories respectively, require disjointness after \\(v\\), departure on opposite sides of \\(v\\), and every right-prefix crossing at \\(d_1\\) strictly right of the left terminal coordinate. The probability of all requirements is at most\n\\[\nC_\\eta(1+s)^{-3/4+\\eta}\n\\]\nfor every \\(\\eta&gt;0\\). At a shared turn this applies vacuously with \\(s=1\\), or for \\(s\\) sufficiently below adjacent arm heights that distant endpoint adjustments do not affect the tests. Extending the two arms below to the same top port duplicates only their highest center, producing a fixed factor \\(\\kappa\\), absorbed into an absolute constant; no shape-dependent trimming is used.</p><p>(iii) For every sufficiently small fixed \\(\\xi&gt;0\\), some \\(c,C&gt;0\\) give\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\omega\\text{ rooted at a fixed center}\\\\\nR\\le\\operatorname{diam}\\omega\\le2R,\\ \nL(\\omega)\\le R^{4/3-\\xi}}}\n\\kappa^{L(\\omega)}\n\\le Ce^{-R^c}.\n\\]\nAll atoms use the actual support lattice.</p>", "proof": "<p>These are the calibrated inputs cited in Section 9. The finite length window is not replaced by a pointwise endpoint estimate. The opening height, diameter, and length-deficit estimates come from [15, Proposition 4.1 and Lemma 4.2]; atoms from its Lemma 7.2; the censored survival test and shared-turn application from Lemma 7.4 and Section 7.3; and the absolute short-path estimate from Theorem 7.1.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Lemma 9.3 (Truncated length reward)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\mathbb E[L;Y\\le2H]\\le H^{d_*-a+o(1)} \\qquad(H\\to\\infty). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the calibrated strict irreducible law of Proposition 13. Set\n\\[\nd_*=\\frac43,\\qquad a=\\frac34.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathbb E[L;Y\\le2H]\\le H^{d_*-a+o(1)}\n\\qquad(H\\to\\infty).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal L_h\\) be the total length-weighted mass of height-\\(h\\) strict bridges from one fixed port, terminal port summed. Insert a length-marked irreducible of height at most \\(2H\\) between two arbitrary bridges of heights at most \\(H\\). Unique factorization gives\n\\[\n\\left(\\sum_{0\\le u\\le H}B_u\\right)^2\n \\mathbb E[L;Y\\le2H]\n\\le\\sum_{0\\le h\\le4H}\\mathcal L_h.\n\\]\nAn output may have several eligible marked pieces, but their lengths sum to at most its full length. The bridge sum is order \\(H^a\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(h\\), first restrict diameter to \\(h\\log^2h\\). Translate its source to order \\(h\\) positions and use the calibrated convex-box length bound in a box of size \\(O(h\\log^2h)\\), obtaining\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_h\\le h^{13/12+o(1)}\n\\]\nfor these bridges. Larger diameters are negligible by exponential strip cutoff and the area bound on length. Bounded strip heights also have exponential diameter tails, by bounded extension to a sufficiently large fixed height. Therefore\n\\[\n\\sum_{h\\le4H}\\mathcal L_h\\le H^{a+d_*+o(1)}.\n\\]\nDivide by \\(H^{2a}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Lemma 9.4 (Weak order from separated ports)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For \\(S\\ge2\\), \\( \\Pi_g(S)\\le C(g/S)^{1/4}\\sqrt{\\log(2+S)}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Start two independent upward irreducible sequences at bottom ports distance \\(g\\ge1\\) apart.</p><p class=\"li\">Assign left and right roles by their starts.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Pi_g(S)\\) be the probability that their endpoint order is preserved weakly at every common renewal height strictly below \\(S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the calibrated finite package of Proposition 13, independently of the off-axis construction used in Proposition 7.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(S\\ge2\\),\n\\[\n\\Pi_g(S)\\le\nC(g/S)^{1/4}\\sqrt{\\log(2+S)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Positive common levels form renewals. Let \\((T,W)\\) record one common step's height and signed separation change. Their renewal mass is\n\\[\nV_j=B_j^2\\asymp(1+j)^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nThe first common return has mass one. Write \\(b=1/2\\); then\n\\[\n1-\\mathbb Ee^{-T/r}\\asymp r^{-b}.\n\\]\nExchanging sequences sends \\(W\\) to \\(-W\\) and preserves \\((T,W)\\), so height damping preserves displacement symmetry.</p><p>First prove\n\\[\n\\Pr(|W|&gt;r)\\le Cr^{-b},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E\\min(1,|W|/r)\\gtrsim r^{-b}.\n\\]\nPairs in a height bin near small fixed \\(c_0r\\) have total mass \\(\\gtrsim r^b\\) within individual diameter \\(r/3\\), by tightness, and therefore all \\(W\\)-increments are bounded by \\(r\\). Comparing with the restricted renewal sum proves the tail upper bound.</p><p>For the lower expectation, construct at every \\(h\\asymp r\\) strict bridges of mass \\(\\gtrsim h^{-1/4}\\), with displacement of fixed sign and magnitude at least \\(ch\\), using only the calibrated half-plane continuation of [12, Section 11, Recoverable half-plane growth]. That continuation uses inward pure normals of dot product \\(1/2\\), goes from initial scale \\(r_0\\) to the exact second-normal wall at \\(S\\ge Kr_0\\), has free terminal port, mass\n\\[\ncS^{-1/4}(S/r_0)^{-C},\n\\]\ndiameter at most \\(C'S\\), prescribed relative transverse tolerance, and recoverable joining cuts.</p><p>Apply it with normals \\(y,u\\) at angle \\(\\pi/3\\), initial scale \\(c_*h\\), and each exact \\(u\\)-wall in\n\\[\n[(2-2\\delta_*)h,(2-\\delta_*)h].\n\\]\nPreselect a small tolerance and initial-scale fraction for the later \\(u,y\\) continuation; its diameter constant is then independent of \\(\\delta_*\\). Choose \\(\\delta_*\\) small in terms of that constant, then choose the first tolerance and \\(c_*\\) still smaller.</p><p>The first continuation gains \\(y\\)-height with slope \\(1/2\\), stays in \\(0&lt;y&lt;h\\), ends with remaining height between \\(\\delta_*h/4\\) and \\(2\\delta_*h\\), and has lateral displacement at least \\(ch\\). Every target wall has mass \\(\\gtrsim h^{-1/4}\\). Extract the prefix at the lowest qualifying singly crossed \\(u\\)-cut in the bin, using confinement and terminal-position tests. Summed target-level mass is \\(\\gtrsim h^{3/4}\\), and suffix mass per prefix is at most \\(\\sum_{j\\le Ch}B_j\\le Ch^{3/4}\\). Distinct prefixes thus have mass \\(\\gtrsim1\\).</p><p>Continue each prefix with normals \\(u,y\\) to exactly \\(y=h\\), with mass \\(\\gtrsim h^{-1/4}\\), diameter \\(O(\\delta_*h)\\), and all centers beyond its joining \\(u\\)-cut. The choices preserve displacement and avoidance of \\(y=0\\). The first-qualifying rule recovers the cut.</p><p>Reflect for the second bridge. Their product mass with total absolute displacement difference at least \\(ch\\) is \\(\\gtrsim h^{-1/2}=h^{b-1}\\). Subadditivity of \\(\\min(1,|\\cdot|/r)\\) over common steps and renewal insertion cost\n\\[\n\\max_{s\\le h}\\sum_{i+j=s}V_iV_j=O(1)\n\\]\ngive the expectation lower bound. Tail truncation and symmetrization now yield\n\\[\n\\Pr(cr\\le|W|\\le r)\\gtrsim r^{-b},\n\\qquad\n1-|\\varphi_W(u)|^2\\gtrsim|u|^b.\n\\]\nLattice Fourier inversion gives atoms \\(Ck^{-1/b}\\) for \\(k\\)-step sums.</p><p>Kill the process at an independent exponential height \\(E\\) of mean \\(S\\). Its symmetric subprobability displacement kernel is\n\\[\nQ_S(w)=\\mathbb E[e^{-T/S};W=w],\\qquad\nq_0=1-\\sum_wQ_S(w)\\asymp S^{-b}.\n\\]\nLet \\(p\\) be the probability that all partial sums stay at least \\(-g\\) before death, and let \\(N_g\\) count indices, including zero, within \\(g\\) of the global minimum. Then\n\\[\n\\mathbb EN_g=p^2/q_0.\n\\]\nIndeed if \\(a_j(g)\\) is the subprobability mass of \\(j\\) steps staying above \\(-g\\), then\n\\(p=q_0\\sum_{j\\ge0}a_j(g)\\). At a candidate with \\(i\\) past and \\(j\\) future steps, reverse and negate the past. Symmetry and independence give mass \\(q_0a_i(g)a_j(g)\\). Summation counts all qualifying times, including ties.</p><p>Since\n\\[\nQ_S^{*k}(w)\\le\\Pr(W_1+\\cdots+W_k=w),\n\\]\nthe expected number \\(L_A\\) of visits to any interval \\(A\\) of width \\(2g\\), uniformly over starts, is\n\\[\n\\mathbb EL_A\\le1+\\sum_{k\\ge1}\\min\\{1,Cgk^{-1/b}\\}\n\\le Cg^b.\n\\]\nMarkov's inequality and restarting at visit-count thresholds give an exponential tail at scale \\(g^b\\).</p><p>Anchor a radius-\\(g\\) interval at each alive index. The earliest near-minimum index captures every near-minimum visit in its interval. Do not condition on that retrospectively chosen index; union bound over all indices, whose survival probabilities sum to \\(1/q_0\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\Pr(N_g\\ge t)\\le\n\\min\\{1,(C/q_0)e^{-ct/g^b}\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb EN_g\\le Cg^b\\log(2+1/q_0).\n\\]\nThe factorization gives\n\\[\np\\le C(g/S)^{b/2}\\sqrt{\\log(2+S)}.\n\\]\nPreserving order below \\(S\\) preserves it below \\(E\\) on \\(\\{E&lt;S\\}\\), so independence gives\n\\[\n(1-e^{-1})\\Pi_g(S)\\le p.\n\\]\nUse \\(b/2=1/4\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For arbitrarily small fixed \\(\\eta>0\\), \\( \\mathcal M_g(H) \\le C_\\eta g^{1/4}H^{d_*-2a+O(\\eta)} \\qquad(H\\ge g/\\log^2g). \\) Smaller height scales contribute negligibly.", "statement": "<p>Dyadic marked-arm length estimate; original equation (9.1).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use prescribed half-plane gap \\(g\\) and the calibrated package of Proposition 13.</p><p class=\"li\">Split an arch at a chosen highest vertex. Extend both arms to the directly adjacent top wall port, duplicating just that vertex, to obtain strict downward bridges.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(h(\\gamma)\\) be that top wall's integral height.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(d_*=4/3\\), \\(a=3/4\\), and, for dyadic \\(H\\),\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal M_g(H)=\n  \\sum_{\\substack{\\gamma:0\\to g\\text{ a half-plane arch}\\\\\n                  H\\le h(\\gamma)&lt;2H}}\n  L(\\gamma)\\kappa^{L(\\gamma)}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For arbitrarily small fixed \\(\\eta&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal M_g(H)\n\\le C_\\eta g^{1/4}H^{d_*-2a+O(\\eta)}\n\\qquad(H\\ge g/\\log^2g). \\tag{9.1}\n\\]\nSmaller height scales contribute negligibly. Consequently\n\\[\nK_g\\mathbb EL\\le g^{1/12+o(1)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The product of the two arm weights differs from arch weight by a fixed factor from the duplicated highest center. Count arm shapes from a reference top port, impose equal heights and lateral difference \\(g\\), and translate the first bottom endpoint to its prescribed position. There is no additional placement sum.</p><p>The top uses Proposition 13(ii)'s shared-turn test; the separated bottom ports use Proposition 15. Mark an irreducible on one arm with its length, reserving independent pieces away from both ends and the mark for endpoint matching. Sum the two choices of rewarded arm.</p><p>For \\(H&lt;g/\\log^2g\\), strip aspect makes contributions negligible because one bridge has diameter order at least \\(g\\). For remaining dyads, diameters above \\(CH\\log^2H\\) cost \\(O_A(H^{-A})\\) for every fixed \\(A\\), including length weights by area. Thus polynomial length costs may be paid in superpolynomial discards.</p><p>Let the arm counts \\(k_i\\) lie in dyads \\(K_i\\), and \\(K=\\max K_i\\). At fixed counts, arm masses are product independent-piece laws. Discard \\(k_i&gt;H^{a+\\eta}\\) by height damping; \\(k_i\\le2H\\). Expand length by marking one piece on its arm. On the other arm, if \\(k_i\\le H^{a-\\eta}\\), also mark a jump above \\(H^{1-\\eta/(2a)}\\), upper-bounding by choices. Without one, height \\(H\\) costs superpolynomially little by exponential weighting of truncated jumps. At larger count, designate a middle index instead.</p><p>Put designated index distances from top and bottom into dyads \\(I_i,J_i\\), counting from one; then \\(\\max(I_i,J_i)\\asymp K_i\\). Proposition 14 bounds a length piece by \\(H^{d_*-a+o(1)}\\), while the jump costs \\(CH^{-a+O(\\eta)}\\). The number of choices per side is at most a constant times \\(m_i=\\min(I_i,J_i)\\). In the deterministic middle-index case, \\(m_iH^{-a+\\eta}\\gtrsim1\\). The combined reward-and-designation budget is therefore\n\\[\nC_\\eta H^{d_*-2a+O(\\eta)}m_1m_2.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(r_i\\) be designated indices and write\n\\[\nu_i=r_i-1,\\quad v_i=k_i-r_i,\\quad\nu=\\min_i u_i,\\quad v=\\min_i v_i.\n\\]\nUse the first \\(\\lfloor u/16\\rfloor\\) pieces of each arm for the top test and the last \\(\\lfloor v/16\\rfloor\\) for the bottom. They avoid marks and are disjoint since \\(u+v\\le k_i-1\\). On a maximal-count arm, at least \\(15k_{\\max}/16-1\\) indices remain after these blocks and its mark. Reserve the first \\(\\lfloor k_{\\max}/4\\rfloor\\) unused indices, in their original order, for the planar atom, and condition on all other draws. Reserved pieces need not be consecutive: they remain independent and their displacements add.</p><p>Let \\(I=\\min I_i\\), \\(J=\\min J_i\\). If \\(u&lt;16\\) or \\(v&lt;16\\), the corresponding scale is bounded and that test is omitted at constant cost. Otherwise top and bottom testing blocks have orders \\(I,J\\). Bounded \\(k_{\\max}\\) is absorbed in constants.</p><p>Take\n\\[\nS_I=I^{1/a}H^{-C_0\\eta},\\qquad\nS_J=J^{1/a}H^{-C_0\\eta},\n\\]\nwith fixed sufficiently large \\(C_0\\). Except for superpolynomial errors by height damping, the blocks reach these thresholds when they are at least two. The thresholds are far below arm height. Shared-turn crosscuts and Proposition 13(ii) give top cost \\(C_\\eta I^{-1}H^{O(\\eta)}\\). Disjoint strip crosscuts at the bottom and Proposition 15 give\n\\[\nCg^{b/2}J^{-b/(2a)}H^{O(\\eta)},\n\\qquad b=1/2.\n\\]\nFor thresholds below two, enlarge \\(O(\\eta)\\) and use cost one. Reversed bridge shapes are independent by bridge symmetry; bottom tests are placed at the prescribed bottom ports, with actual placement enforced by the increment equalities, so they do not consume middle increments.</p><p>Reserve order \\(K\\) draws on the larger arm for equal height and prescribed lateral difference. Proposition 13(i) gives cost \\(CK^{-2/a}\\), independently of tests and marks.</p><p>There are at most \\(CK_1K_2\\) count pairs. With \\(p'=b/(2a)=1/3\\), the count-scale factors obey\n\\[\nK_1K_2K^{-2/a}\n \\prod_i\\min(I_i,J_i)\\,I^{-1}J^{-p'}\n\\le\nCK^{-2/a}\n \\max(I_1,I_2)\\max(J_1,J_2)J^{1-p'}\n\\le C.\n\\]\nThe deterministic middle-index factor is a counting allowance, not a probability bound on its unconditioned piece.</p><p>Including polylogarithmic bin sums proves (9.1). Since \\(d_*-2a=-1/6&lt;0\\), sum over height dyads and choose \\(\\eta\\) arbitrarily small:\n\\[\n\\sum_H\\mathcal M_g(H)\\le g^{1/12+o(1)}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "Proposition 10.3 (Polynomial-vacuum rewards), together with the finite inputs stated in Section 10", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The finite polynomial-vacuum construction [14, Theorem 1.1 and Lemma 8.2] gives \\( b_h=h^{-1/4+o(1)},\\qquad \\sum_e b_h^{(L)}(e)=h^{13/12+o(1)}, \\) with exponential strip aspect cutoff.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the arrays \\(b_h(e)\\), \\(b_h^{(L)}(e)\\) of Theorem 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A_h\\) be the raw critical mass of nontrivial paths from the fixed bottom source back to the bottom wall, staying in the height-\\(h\\) strip, terminal port summed.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(i\\) be the positive-height irreducible probability, recording height and transverse displacement.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(i^{(L)}(h,e)\\) sum \\(L(\\gamma)\\kappa^{L(\\gamma)}\\) over irreducibles at those coordinates.</p><p class=\"li\">Convolution is in both height and offset.</p><p class=\"li\">In this proposition and Propositions 18–20, \\(J\\) denotes one piece's height; put\n  \\[\n  \\alpha=1/4,\\qquad\\beta=3/4,\\qquad d_*=4/3.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The finite polynomial-vacuum construction [14, Theorem 1.1 and Lemma 8.2] gives\n\\[\nb_h=h^{-1/4+o(1)},\\qquad\n\\sum_e b_h^{(L)}(e)=h^{13/12+o(1)},\n\\]\nwith exponential strip aspect cutoff. Its flat-strip flux identity is\n\\[\nb_h+\\cos(3\\pi/8)A_h=1.\n\\]</p><p>The reward input [15, Proposition 10.1] gives\n\\[\nb=\\sum_{k\\ge0}i^{*k},\\qquad\nb^{(L)}=b*i^{(L)}*b,\n\\]\n\\[\ni(J&gt;r)=r^{-\\beta+o(1)},\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_i[L;J\\le r]\\le r^{d_*-\\beta+o(1)},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_i[Le^{-J/r}]=r^{d_*-\\beta+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThe discounted lower reward is retained on pieces of height at most \\(r\\log^2r\\) and lateral excursion at most \\(r\\log^4r\\). Reflection makes \\(b_h(e)\\) symmetric in \\(e\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The finite mass, moment, cutoff, and flux statements are the inputs from [14] specified in Section 10. The exact renewal-reward identities and irreducible reward estimates are [15, Proposition 10.1]. Heights and offsets add under concatenation, so the two convolution identities hold for these arrays.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 18", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( b_h(y)=\\sum_{u\\le y<v}J_h(u,v). \\) Consequently, \\( \\sup_e b_h(e)\\le t_h,\\qquad \\sum_e|b_h(e+1)-b_h(e)|\\le2t_h. \\) Also \\( t_h\\le C(A_{h+1}-A_h), \\) and, for fixed \\(0<c_1<c_2<\\infty\\) …", "statement": "<p>Two-bridge endpoint variation; original location: Section 10.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 17's strip setting.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(t_h\\) be the total critical product mass of two vertex-disjoint bridges with adjacent starts \\(0,1\\), in that order.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(J_h(u,v)\\), \\(u&lt;v\\), refine this mass by ordered top endpoints.</p><p class=\"li\">Differences of \\(b_h(\\cdot)\\) are along its actual row coset.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nb_h(y)=\\sum_{u\\le y&lt;v}J_h(u,v).\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\sup_e b_h(e)\\le t_h,\\qquad\n\\sum_e|b_h(e+1)-b_h(e)|\\le2t_h.\n\\]\nAlso\n\\[\nt_h\\le C(A_{h+1}-A_h),\n\\]\nand, for fixed \\(0&lt;c_1&lt;c_2&lt;\\infty\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{c_1H\\le j\\le c_2H}t_j\n\\le Cb_{\\lfloor c_1H\\rfloor}\n=H^{-\\alpha+o(1)}\n\\]\nas an upper bound.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take a spectator bridge from \\(1\\) to top coordinate \\(y'\\), and grow an active exit from \\(0\\) with phase \\(e^{i\\sigma W}\\), \\(\\sigma=3/8\\), stopping just before first contact with spectator vertices. Take also the reversed-role balance, multiplied by\n\\[\ns_0=e^{6i\\theta},\\qquad\\theta=\\pi/8.\n\\]\nRevisit pairs cancel. Exponential lateral cutoff justifies convergence in the horizontally unbounded strip.</p><p>At first contact, the three arms from \\(0,1,y'\\) form a tripod with central weight included once, with bijections from both contact sums. Before turning at the center, their phases satisfy\n\\[\ne^{i\\sigma W_{y'}}\n=e^{i\\theta}e^{i\\sigma W_0}\n=e^{-i\\theta}e^{i\\sigma W_1}.\n\\]\nContact terms cancel because \\(s_0=-e^{-2i\\theta}\\).</p><p>Remaining active arches in the first balance go only left; in the second, only right, by separation from the spectator and the adjacent starting port. Their combined phases are both \\(\\ell=e^{3i\\theta}\\). Project by\n\\[\nX\\longmapsto\n\\frac{\\operatorname{Im}(\\ell^{-1}X)}\n     {\\operatorname{Im}(\\ell^{-1})}.\n\\]\nThis kills arches and gives coefficients \\(1,-1\\) on bridge terms:\n\\[\nb_h(y'-1)-b_h(y')\n=\\sum_{u&lt;y'}J_h(u,y')-\\sum_{v&gt;y'}J_h(y',v).\n\\]\nSumming up to \\(y'=y\\) gives the representation. Its supremum and total-variation consequences follow.</p><p>For \\(t_h\\le C(A_{h+1}-A_h)\\), reflect so adjacent starts lie on the old strip's top. Join them by the bounded minimal arch in one added layer, and translate horizontally so the resulting bottom arch starts at the fixed source, using ordered ends. A strip-exchanging lattice isometry supplies any required offset. Recovery has bounded multiplicity: the old top has exactly the two adjacent joined crossings, prescribed before translation. The output belongs to the added-layer arch difference.</p><p>Finally telescope this inequality and use the flat-strip flux identity of Proposition 17 to obtain the stated height-summed bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 19", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "At every sufficiently large integral height, uniformly over admissible offsets, \\( b_h(e)\\ge h^{-1-\\alpha-o(1)} \\qquad (e\\in\\mathbb Z+h/2,\\ |e|\\le h^{1-\\delta}). \\)", "statement": "<p>All-height pointwise mass lower bound; original location: Section 10.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the polynomial-vacuum bridge arrays and fix \\(\\delta&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At every sufficiently large integral height, uniformly over admissible offsets,\n\\[\nb_h(e)\\ge h^{-1-\\alpha-o(1)}\n\\qquad\n(e\\in\\mathbb Z+h/2,\\ |e|\\le h^{1-\\delta}).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First take even \\(h\\). For \\(\\gamma\\in\\mathcal B_h(e)\\), let \\(N_h(\\gamma)\\) count integers\n\\(j\\in[h/8,h/7]\\) such that \\(j,2j,h/2+j\\) are all singly crossed. Set \\(k=h/2-j\\). Splitting at these three levels gives four bridges of heights \\(j,j,k,k\\), so\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal B_h(e)}\n N_h(\\gamma)\\kappa^{L(\\gamma)}\n=\n\\sum_{h/8\\le j\\le h/7}\n (b_j*b_j*b_k*b_k)(e),\n\\]\nwhere these convolutions are only in offset.</p><p>Put \\(\\delta_0=\\min(\\delta,1/2)\\). Proposition 18 implies that all but \\(o(h)\\) internal levels satisfy\n\\[\nt_j\\le h^{-1-\\alpha+\\delta_0/8}.\n\\]\nThe external height \\(h\\) remains arbitrary.</p><p>Let \\(A=b_j(\\cdot)*b_k(\\cdot)\\). Its total is \\(b_jb_k\\), concentrated on\n\\(|x|\\le Ch\\log^2h\\) up to negligible tails. Its supremum and variation are bounded by \\(t_jb_k\\) and \\(2t_jb_k\\). By symmetry,\n\\[\n\\sum_xA(x)A(e-x)\n=\\sum_xA(x)A(x-e)\n\\ge\\|A\\|_2^2-2|e|t_j^2b_k^2,\n\\]\nand concentration gives\n\\[\n\\|A\\|_2^2\\ge h^{-1-o(1)}(b_jb_k)^2.\n\\]\nFor good \\(j\\), the subtracted term is negligible. Summing produces first moment at least \\(h^{-4\\alpha-o(1)}\\).</p><p>For the second moment, diagonal terms total at most \\(h^{-4\\alpha+o(1)}\\), using \\(\\sup b_j\\le t_j\\) on one factor. For \\(j&lt;j'\\), split at the union of both triples. There are seven bridges: four of heights comparable to \\(h\\), including \\(j\\), and three of heights \\(\\Delta,2\\Delta,\\Delta\\), with \\(\\Delta=j'-j\\). Bound pointwise by the supremum at \\(j\\) and total masses of all others. Since\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\Delta\\le h}b_\\Delta^2b_{2\\Delta}\n\\le h^{1-3\\alpha+o(1)},\n\\]\nsummation of \\(t_j\\) gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal B_h(e)}\n N_h(\\gamma)^2\\kappa^{L(\\gamma)}\n\\le h^{1-7\\alpha+o(1)}.\n\\]\nWeighted Cauchy–Schwarz yields\n\\[\nb_h(e)\\ge\n\\frac{\\left(\\sum_\\gamma N_h(\\gamma)\\kappa^{L(\\gamma)}\\right)^2}\n     {\\sum_\\gamma N_h(\\gamma)^2\\kappa^{L(\\gamma)}}\n\\ge h^{-1-\\alpha-o(1)}.\n\\]</p><p>Odd heights follow by a bounded one-layer extension from even height with the appropriate half-step endpoint shift, using a lower-bound window with a smaller positive \\(\\delta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 20", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Uniformly over admissible sublinear offsets, \\( K_h(e)\\ge h^{-2\\alpha-o(1)} \\qquad(|e|\\le h^{1-\\delta}), \\) and \\( \\sum_{j\\le2H}\\sup_xK_j(x) \\le H^{2\\beta-1+o(1)}. \\) Moreover …", "statement": "<p>Kernel insertion and endpoint reward estimates; original location: Section 10.3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the polynomial-vacuum setting and exponents of Proposition 17.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(K=b*b\\), convolution in both height and offset.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\delta&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly over admissible sublinear offsets,\n\\[\nK_h(e)\\ge h^{-2\\alpha-o(1)}\n\\qquad(|e|\\le h^{1-\\delta}),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\le2H}\\sup_xK_j(x)\n\\le H^{2\\beta-1+o(1)}.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\nb_h^{(L)}(e)\\ge h^{d_*+\\beta-2-o(1)}\n\\qquad(|e|\\le h^{1-\\delta}),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_{H\\le h\\le2H}\\sup_e b_h^{(L)}(e)\n\\le H^{d_*+\\beta-1+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThe lower bounds hold at every sufficiently large height.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the lower bound on \\(K_h(e)\\), sum over factor heights\n\\(h/3\\le l\\le2h/3\\) and offsets \\(|x|\\le h^{1-\\eta}\\), with arbitrarily small \\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;\\delta/2\\). Proposition 19 supplies both factors' lower bounds in sublinear power windows. Send \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\).</p><p>For the upper bound, order factor heights as larger \\(l\\) and smaller \\(r\\). Apart from zero total height, use\n\\[\n\\sup b_l(\\cdot)\\le t_l,\\qquad\n\\sum_{r\\le l}b_r\\le l^{\\beta+o(1)}.\n\\]\nSum dyadically and use Proposition 18. Since \\(\\beta-\\alpha&gt;0\\), this gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\le2H}\\sup_xK_j(x)\\le H^{2\\beta-1+o(1)}.\n\\]</p><p>The exact insertion is\n\\[\nb^{(L)}=K*i^{(L)}.\n\\]\nFor a lower bound, take the middle reward piece with height at most\n\\(m\\log^2m\\) and absolute displacement at most \\(m\\log^4m\\), where\n\\(m=h^{1-\\epsilon}\\) and \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\) is small and fixed. Proposition 17's discounted first moment and retained lower reward give available mass at least\n\\(m^{d_*-\\beta-o(1)}\\). The remaining \\(K\\)-height is comparable to \\(h\\); since\n\\[\nm\\log^4m\\le h^{1-\\epsilon/2}\n\\]\nfor large \\(h\\), the remaining offset is still in a sublinear power window. Apply the \\(K\\) lower bound and let \\(\\epsilon\\downarrow0\\).</p><p>For the summed upper bound, convolve the summed \\(K\\) supremum estimate with the truncated \\(i^{(L)}\\) upper reward up to height \\(2H\\). Their exponents add to \\(d_*+\\beta-1\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [2, 3, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [6, 1, "cite"], [6, 8, "cite"], [6, 9, "cite"], [6, 10, "cite"], [6, 11, "cite"], [6, 14, "cite"], [6, 15, "cite"], [6, 28, "cite"], [7, 9, "cite"], [7, 14, "cite"], [7, 24, "cite"], [8, 0, "cite"], [8, 1, "cite"], [8, 14, "cite"], [8, 25, "cite"], [8, 26, "cite"], [8, 27, "cite"], [9, 1, "cite"], [9, 13, "cite"], [9, 28, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [10, 12, "cite"], [11, 9, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [12, 29, "cite"], [13, 1, "cite"], [13, 29, "cite"], [14, 0, "cite"], [14, 34, "cite"], [15, 14, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 14, "cite"], [16, 32, "cite"], [17, 24, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [18, 22, "cite"], [18, 23, "cite"], [19, 21, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [20, 23, "cite"], [21, 17, "part"], [22, 17, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [23, 17, "cite"], [24, 26, "cite"], [25, 26, "cite"], [25, 27, "cite"], [26, 0, "cite"], [27, 0, "cite"], [28, 31, "cite"], [29, 30, "cite"], [29, 31, "cite"], [30, 27, "cite"], [30, 31, "cite"], [31, 27, "cite"], [32, 2, "cite"], [32, 3, "cite"], [32, 35, "cite"], [33, 32, "cite"], [33, 35, "cite"], [34, 32, "cite"], [34, 35, "cite"], [35, 2, "cite"], [36, 37, "cite"], [36, 39, "cite"], [37, 4, "cite"], [37, 5, "cite"], [37, 38, "cite"], [37, 39, "cite"], [38, 4, "cite"], [38, 5, "cite"], [38, 39, "cite"], [39, 4, "cite"], [39, 5, "cite"]]});
